window.GUIDE_HTML={"fbs-physiology": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Homeostasis &amp; control systems</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Homeostasis</b> = keeping the <b>internal environment (ECF)</b> constant (Claude Bernard’s “milieu intérieur”).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Negative feedback</span> = response <i>opposes</i> the change → stability. Most body systems (BP via baroreceptors, temperature, blood glucose, PTH–Ca²⁺, ADH–osmolarity).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Positive feedback</span> = response <i>amplifies</i> the change. Physiologic examples: <span class=\"hy\">LH surge (estrogen), oxytocin in labor (Ferguson reflex), clotting cascade, action potential upstroke (Na⁺ channel opening), lactation</span>. Pathologic: “vicious cycle” in hemorrhagic shock (cardiac failure → ↓ perfusion → worse failure).</li>\n<li><b>Feedforward</b> = anticipatory response before the variable changes (e.g., ↑ HR before exercise, cephalic phase of digestion).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Gain = correction ÷ remaining error</span>. Higher gain = better control. Example: BP pushed +100, baroreflex returns to +25 → correction 75 / error 25 → gain = −3 (negative = negative feedback). Temperature control has very high gain (~−33).</li>\n<li><b>Baroreceptor reflex</b>: carotid sinus (CN IX) &amp; aortic arch (CN X) stretch → ↑ BP ⇒ ↑ firing ⇒ ↑ vagal, ↓ sympathetic ⇒ ↓ HR, vasodilation. <span class=\"hy\">Fast, short-term BP control; resets in chronic HTN.</span> Carotid massage ↑ firing ⇒ ↓ HR.</li>\n<li>Chemoreceptors (carotid/aortic bodies) sense ↓ PO₂, ↑ PCO₂, ↓ pH.</li>\n<li>Kidneys = long-term BP control (pressure natriuresis, RAAS); infinite gain over time.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Body fluid compartments</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Compartment</th><th>% body weight</th><th>Fraction</th><th>Measured with</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Total body water (TBW)</b></td><td class=\"hy\">≈ 60% (60–40–20 rule)</td><td>—</td><td>D₂O, tritiated water, antipyrine</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ICF</td><td>40%</td><td class=\"hy\">2/3 of TBW</td><td>TBW − ECF</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ECF</td><td>20%</td><td class=\"hy\">1/3 of TBW</td><td>Inulin, mannitol, ²²Na</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Interstitial fluid</td><td>15%</td><td class=\"hy\">3/4 of ECF</td><td>ECF − plasma</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Plasma</td><td>5%</td><td class=\"hy\">1/4 of ECF</td><td>Evans blue, ¹²⁵I-albumin</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li>Blood volume = plasma volume ÷ (1 − Hct). TBW is <b>lower in women, obese, elderly</b> (fat has little water); higher in infants (~75%).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Main ions: ECF = Na⁺, Cl⁻, HCO₃⁻. ICF = K⁺, Mg²⁺, phosphates, proteins.</span> Plasma has more protein than interstitial fluid (Gibbs–Donnan effect: slightly more cations in plasma).</li>\n<li><b>Osmolarity</b> (osm/L solution) vs <b>osmolality</b> (osm/kg water); normal ≈ <span class=\"hy\">285–295 mOsm/kg</span>. Plasma Osm ≈ 2[Na⁺] + glucose/18 + BUN/2.8.</li>\n<li><b>Tonicity</b> = effective osmolarity (only <i>non-penetrating</i> solutes count). <span class=\"hy\">Urea is an ineffective osmole</span> (crosses membranes) → isosmotic urea solution is <i>hypotonic</i> → cells swell.</li>\n<li>Cell in <b>hypotonic</b> solution → water in → swells/lyses. <b>Hypertonic</b> → water out → shrinks (crenation). 0.9% NaCl &amp; 5% dextrose (initially) are isotonic.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Water always moves toward higher osmolarity; disturbances start in the ECF, and the ICF changes only secondarily by osmosis.</span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Darrow–Yannet diagrams (volume/osmolarity shifts) — MUST KNOW</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Example</th><th>ECF vol</th><th>ICF vol</th><th>Osmolarity</th><th>Hct / plasma protein</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Isosmotic expansion</td><td>Isotonic saline infusion</td><td>↑</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>↓ / ↓</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Isosmotic contraction</td><td>Diarrhea, burns, vomiting</td><td>↓</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>↑ / ↑</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hyperosmotic expansion</td><td>High NaCl intake, hypertonic saline</td><td>↑</td><td>↓</td><td>↑</td><td>↓ / ↓</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Hyperosmotic contraction</td><td>Sweating, fever, diabetes insipidus</td><td>↓</td><td>↓</td><td>↑</td><td>— / ↑</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Hyposmotic expansion</td><td>SIADH, water intoxication</td><td>↑</td><td>↑</td><td>↓</td><td>— / ↓</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Hyposmotic contraction</td><td>Adrenal insufficiency (↓ aldosterone)</td><td>↓</td><td>↑</td><td>↓</td><td>↑ / ↑</td></tr></table>\n<p><span class=\"mn\">Hct logic:</span> Hct = RBC volume ÷ (RBC + plasma). Losing plasma alone (diarrhea) ↑ Hct. RBCs act like ICF: they shrink when ECF osmolarity ↑ and swell when it ↓, which cancels or adds to the plasma change. Acute hemorrhage (whole blood lost) → ECF ↓ but Hct unchanged at first.</p>\n<ul><li>Heavy sweating → <b>ADH ↑</b>, urine output drops (≈1,500 → 500 mL/day), thirst ↑.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Hyponatremia</span> = too much water relative to Na⁺ (dilutional), e.g., SIADH → brain swelling (confusion, seizures). Correct slowly (osmotic demyelination).</li></ul>\n\n<h3>Starling forces &amp; edema</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Net filtration = Kf [(Pc − Pi) − σ(πc − πi)]</span>. Pc pushes out; πc (plasma oncotic, mainly <b>albumin</b>) pulls in.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Edema causes: ↑ Pc (heart failure, venous obstruction, DVT), ↓ πc (nephrotic syndrome, liver cirrhosis, malnutrition/kwashiorkor), ↑ Kf/permeability (burns, inflammation, sepsis), lymphatic obstruction (filariasis, cancer, node dissection → non-pitting).</span></li>\n<li>Safety factors against edema: low interstitial compliance, ↑ lymph flow (up to 10–50×), washout of interstitial protein.</li>\n<li>Lymph returns ~2–4 L/day of filtered fluid and protein; thoracic duct → left subclavian/IJ junction.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Membrane transport</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Energy</th><th>Carrier?</th><th>Saturable?</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Simple diffusion</td><td>None (down gradient)</td><td>No</td><td>No (linear)</td><td>O₂, CO₂, steroids, ethanol, urea (some)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Facilitated diffusion</td><td>None</td><td>Yes</td><td class=\"hy\">Yes (Tm)</td><td class=\"hy\">GLUT1–5 (GLUT4 insulin-dependent: muscle, fat)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Channels</td><td>None</td><td>Pore</td><td>Can saturate at very high conc.</td><td>Na⁺/K⁺/Ca²⁺ channels, aquaporins</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Primary active</td><td>ATP directly</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Ca²⁺-ATPase (SERCA, PMCA), H⁺/K⁺-ATPase</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Secondary active</td><td>Na⁺ gradient (ATP indirectly)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>SGLT (symport), Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger NCX &amp; Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger NHE (antiport)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Fick’s law</b>: diffusion rate ∝ (area × concentration gradient × permeability)/thickness. Lipid solubility ↑ permeability; size and charge ↓ it. <span class=\"hy\">Ions can’t cross the bilayer because they are charged/hydrated, not lipid-soluble.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase: 3 Na⁺ OUT, 2 K⁺ IN per ATP</span> <span class=\"mn\">(NOKIA: Na⁺ Out, K⁺ In, ATPase)</span>. Basolateral in epithelia. Electrogenic (net +1 out) → contributes a few mV to RMP. Inhibited by <span class=\"hy\">digoxin/ouabain</span>.</li>\n<li><b>Symporter</b> = same direction (SGLT: Na⁺ + glucose IN). <b>Antiporter</b> = opposite directions (NCX: 3 Na⁺ in, 1 Ca²⁺ out; NHE: Na⁺ in, H⁺ out).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Proximal tubule glucose reabsorption order: (1) basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pumps Na⁺ out → (2) low intracellular Na⁺ creates gradient → (3) apical SGLT2 drags glucose in against its gradient → (4) GLUT2 lets glucose out basolaterally.</span></li>\n<li>Glucose transport saturates: renal threshold ≈ <b>200 mg/dL</b>, Tm ≈ 375 mg/min → glucosuria in diabetes.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">SGLT2 inhibitors (-gliflozins)</span> → glucosuria; side effects UTIs, genital yeast infection, euglycemic DKA, volume depletion.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Blocking the Na⁺/K⁺ pump (or ischemia/no ATP) → pump fails FIRST, secondary transport (SGLT) fails second</span>, cells swell (Na⁺ + water in).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box\"><b class=\"t\">Digitalis (digoxin) cascade — classic NBME:</b> blocks Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase → ↑ intracellular Na⁺ → weaker Na⁺ gradient → NCX removes less Ca²⁺ → ↑ intracellular Ca²⁺ → more SR Ca²⁺ → <b>↑ contractility (positive inotropy)</b>. Also ↑ vagal tone (slows AV node). <b>Hypokalemia potentiates toxicity</b> (K⁺ competes for the same site). Toxicity: nausea, yellow-green vision, arrhythmias; antidote = digoxin Fab.</div>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Osmosis</b>: water through aquaporins toward higher osmolarity. Osmotic pressure π = g·C·σ·RT. Reflection coefficient σ: 1 = impermeant (albumin), 0 = freely permeant (urea).</li>\n<li><b>Vesicular transport</b>: endocytosis (phagocytosis, pinocytosis, receptor-mediated via clathrin — LDL), exocytosis (Ca²⁺-triggered, SNAREs), transcytosis.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Resting membrane potential (RMP)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">At rest the membrane is most permeable to K⁺ (K⁺ leak channels) → RMP ≈ −70 to −90 mV, close to E<sub>K</sub>.</span> The Na⁺/K⁺ pump <i>maintains</i> gradients (and adds ~−4 mV) but is not what directly sets RMP.</li>\n<li><b>Nernst equation</b> (single ion at 37 °C): E = (61.5/z) log([out]/[in]).</li>\n<li><b>Goldman (GHK) equation</b>: RMP = weighted average of E for each ion by its permeability → moves toward the ion with highest permeability.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Ion</th><th>[ECF]</th><th>[ICF]</th><th>Equilibrium potential (deck values)</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>K⁺</td><td>4–5 mM</td><td>140–150 mM</td><td>≈ −95 mV</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Na⁺</td><td>140–145 mM</td><td>10–15 mM</td><td>≈ +65 mV</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cl⁻</td><td>~105–110 mM</td><td>~5–10 mM</td><td>≈ −90 mV</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ca²⁺</td><td>~2.5 mM</td><td>~0.0001 mM</td><td>≈ +120 mV</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Hyperkalemia → smaller K⁺ gradient → less K⁺ efflux → depolarized RMP (closer to threshold)</span>. Initially excitable, but sustained depolarization inactivates Na⁺ channels → weakness, <b>peaked T waves, wide QRS, arrhythmias</b>. Treat: <b>IV calcium gluconate</b> (stabilizes membrane), insulin + glucose, β₂-agonists (shift K⁺ in), binders, dialysis.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Hypokalemia → hyperpolarized RMP (further from threshold)</span> → muscle weakness, cramps, ileus, flat T waves, <b>U waves</b>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Calcium sets the THRESHOLD (stabilizes Na⁺ channels).</span> <b>Hypocalcemia</b> → Na⁺ channels open with less depolarization → threshold drops toward RMP → spontaneous firing → <span class=\"hy\">tetany, paresthesias, Chvostek &amp; Trousseau signs</span> (e.g., after thyroidectomy/hypoparathyroidism). <b>Hypercalcemia</b> → threshold ↑ → ↓ excitability, <b>sluggish reflexes</b>, weakness <span class=\"mn\">(stones, bones, groans, thrones, psychiatric overtones)</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Action potentials &amp; conduction</h2>\n<h3>Phases (nerve / skeletal muscle)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Threshold</b> (~−55 mV) → voltage-gated Na⁺ channel <b>activation (m) gates</b> open (positive feedback) → <span class=\"hy\">upstroke = Na⁺ influx</span>; overshoot to ~+35 mV.</li>\n<li>At peak, <span class=\"hy\">Na⁺ inactivation (h) gates close</span> → Na⁺ entry stops; they stay closed until repolarization → <b>absolute refractory period</b>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Repolarization = K⁺ efflux</span> through delayed-rectifier voltage-gated K⁺ channels; slow closure → <b>after-hyperpolarization</b> (undershoot toward E<sub>K</sub>).</li>\n<li><b>Relative refractory period</b>: some Na⁺ channels recovered; a stronger-than-normal stimulus can fire.</li>\n<li><b>All-or-none</b>; stimulus intensity is coded by <b>frequency</b> of APs, not amplitude.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Accommodation</span>: slow depolarization (e.g., hyperkalemia) inactivates Na⁺ channels before threshold → no AP even though Vm passes threshold.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Toxin / drug</th><th>Target</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Tetrodotoxin (pufferfish), saxitoxin (red tide)</td><td>Voltage-gated Na⁺ channel (from outside)</td><td>No upstroke → no AP, paralysis</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Tetraethylammonium (TEA)</td><td>Voltage-gated K⁺ channels (from inside)</td><td>Impaired repolarization → prolonged AP</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Local anesthetics (lidocaine)</td><td>Na⁺ channel (from inside, use-dependent)</td><td>Block conduction; small, myelinated pain fibers first</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ciguatoxin / batrachotoxin</td><td>Hold Na⁺ channels open</td><td>Persistent depolarization</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Conduction velocity</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">↑ velocity with ↑ diameter (↓ internal resistance) and myelination.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Myelin ↓ membrane capacitance and ↑ membrane resistance</span> → less charge needed to change Vm, current spreads farther → <b>saltatory conduction</b> node to node (Na⁺ channels concentrated at nodes of Ranvier).</li>\n<li>Length constant λ = √(Rm/Ri): large λ = signal spreads farther. Time constant τ = Rm × Cm: small τ = faster depolarization.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Demyelination (MS — CNS oligodendrocytes; Guillain–Barré — PNS Schwann cells) ↑ capacitance, current leaks → slowed/blocked conduction.</span></li>\n<li>Fiber types: Aα (largest, fastest: proprioception, motor) &gt; Aβ (touch) &gt; Aγ &gt; Aδ (fast pain, cold) &gt; B (preganglionic autonomic) &gt; C (unmyelinated, slow pain, postganglionic).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Graded vs action potentials</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Graded (local) potential</th><th>Action potential</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Size ∝ stimulus; summate (spatial/temporal)</td><td>All-or-none</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Decay with distance; no refractory period</td><td>Propagated without decrement; refractory period</td></tr>\n<tr><td>EPSP/IPSP, receptor/end-plate potentials</td><td>Axon, muscle fibers</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Rapid-fire NBME pearls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">Na⁺/K⁺ pump: 3 out, 2 in, primary active, basolateral; digoxin &amp; ouabain block it.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">SGLT = secondary active symport (apical); GLUT = facilitated diffusion, no ATP.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">RMP ≈ E<sub>K</sub> because K⁺ permeability dominates; ECF K⁺ is the main determinant of RMP stability.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">TTX blocks Na⁺ (no upstroke); TEA blocks K⁺ (no repolarization).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Diarrhea = isosmotic contraction (↑ Hct). SIADH = hyposmotic expansion. Adrenal insufficiency = hyposmotic contraction. Sweating = hyperosmotic contraction.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Nephrotic syndrome / cirrhosis → ↓ albumin → ↓ πc → edema. Heart failure → ↑ Pc → edema.</li>\n<li>ICF 2/3, ECF 1/3; interstitial 3/4 of ECF, plasma 1/4.</li>\n<li>Positive feedback examples: LH surge, oxytocin, clotting, Na⁺ channel opening in AP upstroke.</li>\n<li>Hypocalcemia → tetany (↓ threshold). Hypercalcemia → sluggish reflexes.</li>\n</ul>", "fbs-cellbio": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Organelles</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Cytosol</b> = fluid portion only; <b>cytoplasm</b> = cytosol + organelles + inclusions (everything inside the plasma membrane except the nucleus).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Nucleus</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Double membrane (outer continuous with RER), <b>nuclear pores</b> (bidirectional transport; mRNA export, protein import via NLS). Blocking pores → mRNA can’t reach ribosomes → no translation.</li>\n<li><b>Nuclear lamina</b> (lamins A, B, C — type V intermediate filaments) maintains shape; LINC complexes connect it to the cytoskeleton. <span class=\"hy\">Lamin A mutation → progeria (Hutchinson–Gilford).</span></li>\n<li><b>Nucleolus</b> = site of <span class=\"hy\">rRNA synthesis (RNA polymerase I)</span> and ribosomal subunit assembly. RNA Pol II = mRNA; Pol III = tRNA, 5S rRNA.</li>\n<li>mRNA path: transcription (nucleus) → capping, splicing, poly-A tail → export via pores → translation on ribosomes.</li>\n<li>Heterochromatin = condensed, inactive (Barr body); euchromatin = open, active.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Ribosomes, RER, Golgi</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Eukaryotic 80S = 60S + 40S (prokaryotic 70S = 50S + 30S → antibiotic targets).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Signal sequence + SRP decide fate</span>: SRP binds the N-terminal signal peptide, pauses translation, docks the ribosome on the RER (SRP receptor) → protein enters ER lumen via translocon (Sec61).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">RER makes secretory, membrane and lysosomal proteins</span> (and N-linked glycosylation begins). <b>Free ribosomes</b> make cytosolic, nuclear, mitochondrial and <span class=\"hy\">peroxisomal</span> proteins.</li>\n<li>Cells rich in RER: plasma cells, pancreatic acinar cells, goblet cells; Nissl bodies in neurons.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">SER: steroid hormone synthesis (adrenal cortex, Leydig cells, gonads), lipid synthesis, detoxification (hepatocyte cytochrome P450), glycogen breakdown (glucose-6-phosphatase), Ca²⁺ storage (sarcoplasmic reticulum in muscle).</span></li>\n<li><b>Golgi</b>: cis (receives from ER) → trans (ships out). Modifies N-glycans, adds <b>O-linked sugars</b> (Ser/Thr), sulfation of GAGs, proteolytic processing, <span class=\"hy\">adds mannose-6-phosphate (M6P) to lysosomal enzymes</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Coat</th><th>Direction</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>COPII</td><td>Anterograde: ER → cis-Golgi <span class=\"mn\">(“2 = forward to Golgi”)</span></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>COPI</td><td>Retrograde: Golgi → ER (and intra-Golgi), KDEL retrieval</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Clathrin</td><td>trans-Golgi → lysosomes; plasma membrane → endosomes (receptor-mediated endocytosis, e.g., LDL)</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box\"><b class=\"t\">Insulin processing (Anki):</b> preproinsulin (RER, signal peptide cleaved) → proinsulin (folds, 3 disulfides) → Golgi/secretory granules: prohormone convertases cut out <b>C-peptide</b> → insulin + C-peptide secreted 1:1. <b>C-peptide</b> distinguishes endogenous insulin (insulinoma, sulfonylureas: ↑ C-peptide) from exogenous insulin (factitious: ↓ C-peptide). ↑ proinsulin → defective convertase processing.</div>\n<h3>Lysosomes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Acid hydrolases (pH ≈ 4.5–5, H⁺-ATPase); digest by endocytosis, phagocytosis, autophagy.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Trafficking: RER synthesis → cis-Golgi GlcNAc-phosphotransferase adds M6P → M6P receptor in trans-Golgi → clathrin vesicle → late endosome → lysosome.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">I-cell disease (mucolipidosis II)</span>: defective GlcNAc-phosphotransferase → no M6P tag → enzymes secreted → <span class=\"hy\">↑ plasma lysosomal enzymes</span>, inclusion bodies, coarse facies, gingival hyperplasia, restricted joints, clouded corneas; AR, fatal in childhood.</li>\n<li>Classic storage diseases (Tay–Sachs, Gaucher, Niemann–Pick, Fabry, Hurler, Hunter, metachromatic leukodystrophy) = a <b>single enzyme</b> deficient; I-cell = <b>all</b> enzymes mis-sorted.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Disease</th><th>Enzyme</th><th>Key feature</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Tay–Sachs</td><td>Hexosaminidase A (GM2)</td><td>Cherry-red macula, no hepatosplenomegaly, onion-skin lysosomes</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Gaucher</td><td>Glucocerebrosidase</td><td>Most common; crumpled tissue-paper macrophages, bone crises, HSM</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Niemann–Pick</td><td>Sphingomyelinase</td><td>Cherry-red macula + HSM, foamy cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fabry (XR)</td><td>α-galactosidase A</td><td>Neuropathic pain, angiokeratomas, renal/cardiac disease</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hurler / Hunter (XR)</td><td>α-L-iduronidase / iduronate sulfatase</td><td>GAG accumulation; corneal clouding in Hurler only <span class=\"mn\">(Hunters aim for the X)</span></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Metachromatic leukodystrophy</td><td>Arylsulfatase A</td><td>Demyelination</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Mitochondria</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Double membrane; inner membrane folds = <b>cristae</b> (↑ surface for ETC/ATP synthase). Matrix: TCA cycle, β-oxidation, own circular DNA.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">mtDNA: ~37 genes, maternal inheritance, heteroplasmy</span> (mixed normal/mutant copies) → variable severity between siblings and tissues; threshold effect.</li>\n<li>Endosymbiotic evidence: own circular DNA, 70S-like ribosomes, binary fission, double membrane, cardiolipin.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Mitochondrial myopathy: ragged-red fibers</span> (Gomori trichrome); lactic acidosis; tissues with high ATP demand (brain, muscle, eye) affected.</li>\n<li>MELAS (encephalomyopathy, lactic acidosis, stroke-like episodes), MERRF (myoclonic epilepsy, ragged-red fibers), <span class=\"hy\">LHON (Leber hereditary optic neuropathy — sudden bilateral vision loss in young men; affected males cannot pass it on)</span>.</li>\n<li>Apoptosis: <span class=\"hy\">cytochrome c release</span> (outer membrane permeabilized by Bax/Bak) → apoptosome.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Peroxisomes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Single membrane; proteins made on <b>free ribosomes</b>, imported via PTS1 (C-terminal SKL) and <b>peroxins (PEX)</b>; replicate by fission; no DNA.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Functions: β-oxidation of very-long-chain fatty acids (VLCFA) and branched-chain (α-oxidation of phytanic acid), plasmalogen synthesis (myelin), bile acid synthesis, H₂O₂ production + catalase breakdown.</span></li>\n<li>Peroxisomal β-oxidation makes heat + H₂O₂ (no ATP); shortened chains go to mitochondria to finish.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Zellweger syndrome</span>: PEX gene defect → no functional peroxisomes; hypotonia, seizures, hepatomegaly, craniofacial anomalies, early death.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy</span>: ABCD1 transporter defect → VLCFA buildup → adrenal insufficiency + progressive demyelination in boys.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Refsum disease</span>: phytanic acid accumulation (α-oxidation defect) → ataxia, retinitis pigmentosa, ichthyosis, neuropathy; restrict dietary phytanic acid (dairy, ruminant fat).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Plasma membrane</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Functions: barrier, transport, signaling, cell recognition/adhesion. Basic structure = <b>phospholipid bilayer</b> (amphipathic: polar heads, nonpolar tails; held by hydrophobic interactions).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Outer leaflet: phosphatidylcholine, sphingomyelin, glycolipids. Inner leaflet: phosphatidylserine (PS), phosphatidylethanolamine, phosphatidylinositol.</span> PS flipping to the outside = “eat me” signal in apoptosis (annexin V detects it).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cholesterol = fluidity buffer</span>: at high temperature ↓ fluidity (restrains tails); at low temperature ↑ fluidity (prevents packing).</li>\n<li>Unsaturated (kinked) fatty acids &amp; short chains ↑ fluidity; saturated &amp; long chains and cold ↓ fluidity (hypothermia → stiff membranes).</li>\n<li><b>Glycolipids</b>: sugars face outside; recognition. <span class=\"hy\">Cholera toxin binds ganglioside GM1</span> → ↑ cAMP → secretory diarrhea.</li>\n<li><b>Proteins</b>: integral (transmembrane α-helices or β-barrels; channels, carriers, receptors), peripheral (non-covalent, e.g., spectrin, cytochrome c), lipid-anchored (Ras via prenylation; GPI-anchored proteins).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">ABO blood type</span>: glycosyltransferases add sugars to H antigen — A adds GalNAc, B adds galactose, <b>O has no active transferase</b> (bare H antigen). Bombay phenotype lacks H.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">ABC transporters (P-glycoprotein/MDR1)</span> pump drugs out using ATP → chemotherapy resistance; at the blood–brain barrier they keep many drugs out of the CNS.</li>\n<li><b>Glycocalyx</b>: carbohydrate coat — protection, recognition, endothelial anti-adhesion, enterocyte brush-border enzymes.</li>\n<li><b>Lipid rafts</b>: cholesterol + sphingolipid-rich, less fluid microdomains for signaling. <b>Caveolae</b> (caveolin) = flask-shaped rafts; endocytosis/transcytosis, abundant in endothelium, adipocytes, smooth muscle; absent in neurons/RBCs.</li>\n<li><b>Polarity</b>: apical vs basolateral domains separated by <span class=\"hy\">tight junctions (claudins, occludin)</span>; classic example = intestinal/renal epithelium.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Cytoskeleton</h2>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Microfilaments (actin)</th><th>Intermediate filaments</th><th>Microtubules</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Diameter</td><td>~7 nm (smallest)</td><td>~10 nm</td><td>~25 nm (largest, hollow)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Subunit</td><td>G-actin → F-actin</td><td>Tissue-specific proteins</td><td>α/β-tubulin dimers, 13 protofilaments</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Energy</td><td class=\"hy\">ATP; treadmilling</td><td>None; very stable</td><td class=\"hy\">GTP; dynamic instability</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Polarity</td><td>Yes (+ barbed)</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (− at centrosome, + at periphery)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Functions</td><td>Microvilli, contractile ring, cell motility, adherens junctions</td><td class=\"hy\">Tensile strength; tumor markers</td><td>Mitotic spindle, cilia/flagella, axonal transport</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Motors</td><td>Myosin</td><td>—</td><td class=\"hy\">Kinesin (+ end, anterograde), dynein (− end, retrograde)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Actin drugs</b>: cytochalasin (blocks polymerization), phalloidin (stabilizes).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Spectrin–ankyrin–band 3–protein 4.1</span> = RBC membrane skeleton. <span class=\"hy\">Hereditary spherocytosis</span> (usually AD): spherocytes without central pallor, ↑ MCHC, splenomegaly, hemolytic anemia, gallstones; <b>osmotic fragility / EMA binding test</b>; splenectomy.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Dystrophin</span> links actin to the dystroglycan complex/ECM in muscle. <span class=\"hy\">Duchenne</span> (X-linked, frameshift deletion → no dystrophin): proximal weakness by age ~5, <b>Gowers sign</b>, calf pseudohypertrophy, ↑ CK, dilated cardiomyopathy. <b>Becker</b>: in-frame deletion → truncated dystrophin, milder, later onset.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Intermediate filament</th><th>Tissue / tumor marker</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Cytokeratin</td><td>Epithelium → carcinomas</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Vimentin</td><td>Mesenchyme (fibroblasts, endothelium) → sarcomas</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Desmin</td><td>Muscle → rhabdo/leiomyosarcoma</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>GFAP</td><td>Astrocytes/glia → gliomas</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Neurofilaments</td><td>Neurons → neuronal tumors</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lamins</td><td>Nuclear envelope (all cells)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Microtubule drugs</span>: <b>vincristine/vinblastine</b> block polymerization; <b>paclitaxel (taxanes)</b> stabilize (block depolymerization) <span class=\"mn\">(“Taxes stabilize society”)</span>; <b>colchicine</b> blocks polymerization (gout, ↓ neutrophil migration); griseofulvin; mebendazole. All arrest mitosis (M phase). <span class=\"hy\">Vincristine → peripheral neuropathy</span> (axonal transport depends on microtubules); vinblastine → bone-marrow suppression.</li>\n<li>Kinesin/dynein: rabies, HSV, polio and tetanus toxin travel <b>retrograde via dynein</b> to the cell body.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cilia: 9+2 axoneme</span> (motile: airway, fallopian tube, ependyma) with <b>dynein arms</b>; basal body = 9 triplets; primary (nonmotile, 9+0) cilia sense (kidney tubule flow, photoreceptors, embryonic node). Ciliopathies: polycystic kidney disease, Bardet–Biedl.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Kartagener syndrome (primary ciliary dyskinesia)</span>: AR dynein arm defect → infertility (immotile sperm; ↑ ectopic pregnancy), recurrent sinusitis, bronchiectasis, <b>situs inversus</b> (~50%); ↓ nasal NO.</li>\n<li><b>Centrosome</b> (pair of centrioles, γ-tubulin) = MTOC; organizes spindle. Kinetochore microtubules attach to chromosomes; polar/interpolar push poles apart; astral anchor to cortex.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Cell cycle</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Phases: <b>G1</b> (growth; variable length) → <b>S</b> (DNA replication: DNA content doubles 2c → 4c, still 2n) → <b>G2</b> (prep, check DNA) → <b>M</b> (prophase, prometaphase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase) → cytokinesis.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">G0</span> = quiescent, out of cycle. Cell types: <b>labile</b> (never G0: skin, gut epithelium, bone marrow, hair follicles), <b>stable/quiescent</b> (G0 but can re-enter: hepatocytes, kidney tubule cells, fibroblasts), <span class=\"hy\">permanent</span> (cannot divide: neurons, skeletal &amp; cardiac muscle, RBCs).</li>\n<li><b>Regulation</b>: <span class=\"hy\">cyclin–CDK complexes</span> drive transitions (cyclin D–CDK4/6 in G1, cyclin E–CDK2 G1/S, cyclin A S, cyclin B–CDK1 = MPF for G2/M). CDKs are constitutive; cyclins rise and fall (ubiquitin–proteasome degradation; APC/C at metaphase→anaphase).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Rb</span> (tumor suppressor) binds and inhibits <b>E2F</b>; cyclin D–CDK4 <b>phosphorylates Rb</b> → releases E2F → G1→S. <span class=\"hy\">HPV E7 binds Rb; E6 degrades p53.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">p53 (“guardian of the genome”)</span>: DNA damage → p53 → <b>p21</b> (CDK inhibitor) → G1 arrest for repair; if irreparable → apoptosis (↑ Bax/PUMA/NOXA). Mutated in &gt;50% of cancers; Li–Fraumeni syndrome (germline).</li>\n<li>Checkpoints: G1/S (DNA damage), G2/M (replication complete, DNA intact), <b>spindle (M) checkpoint</b> (all kinetochores attached) — failure → aneuploidy.</li>\n<li><b>Mitosis</b>: 1 diploid → 2 identical diploid cells. <b>Meiosis</b>: 1 diploid → 4 haploid; <span class=\"hy\">meiosis I separates homologs (reductional), crossing over in prophase I; meiosis II separates sister chromatids</span>. Nondisjunction → trisomy (e.g., Down; risk ↑ with maternal age, mostly meiosis I).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Apoptosis</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Apoptosis</th><th>Necrosis</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"hy\">ATP-dependent, programmed; single cells</td><td>Passive; groups of cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"hy\">Cell shrinks; pyknosis → karyorrhexis → karyolysis; eosinophilic cytoplasm; apoptotic bodies</td><td>Cell swells, membrane ruptures, contents leak</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"hy\">NO inflammation (membrane intact; PS “eat-me” → phagocytosis)</td><td>Inflammation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DNA laddering (internucleosomal cleavage, ~180 bp)</td><td>Random DNA degradation (smear)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Physiologic roles</b>: embryogenesis (interdigital webs, lumen formation), hormone withdrawal (endometrium, menstrual shedding), <span class=\"hy\">thymic negative selection</span>, immune cell deletion, neutrophil turnover, cytotoxic T-cell killing, keeping tissue size constant.</li>\n<li><b>Intrinsic (mitochondrial) pathway</b>: DNA damage, growth-factor withdrawal, stress → p53 → <span class=\"hy\">pro-apoptotic Bax/Bak</span> form pores (BH3-only proteins Bim, Bad, PUMA, NOXA activate) → <span class=\"hy\">cytochrome c release</span> → binds Apaf-1 → apoptosome → <span class=\"hy\">caspase 9 (initiator)</span> → caspase 3/6/7 (executioners). <span class=\"hy\">Bcl-2 and Bcl-xL are anti-apoptotic</span> (keep the outer membrane closed).</li>\n<li><b>Extrinsic (death receptor) pathway</b>: <span class=\"hy\">Fas ligand–Fas (CD95)</span> or TNF–TNFR1 → FADD adaptor → <span class=\"hy\">caspase 8 (initiator)</span> → executioner caspases. Cytotoxic T cells also use <b>perforin + granzyme B</b> (activates caspases directly).</li>\n<li>IAPs/<b>survivin</b> inhibit caspases (overexpressed in tumors). Released Smac/DIABLO blocks IAPs.</li>\n<li>Defective Fas/FasL → <span class=\"hy\">autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome</span> (failure to delete autoreactive lymphocytes).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">BCL-2 overexpression: follicular lymphoma, t(14;18)</span> (BCL-2 moved next to Ig heavy-chain promoter) → cells don’t die. CLL also overexpresses BCL-2 (venetoclax).</li>\n<li><b>Detection</b>: <span class=\"hy\">annexin V (binds externalized PS — early)</span>, TUNEL (DNA fragments), DNA laddering on gel, caspase assays, cytochrome c release.</li>\n<li>Too little apoptosis → cancer, autoimmunity. Too much → neurodegeneration (Alzheimer, Parkinson, ALS), ischemic injury, AIDS (CD4 loss).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Rapid-fire NBME pearls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">I-cell disease = no M6P tag (GlcNAc-phosphotransferase) → enzymes in plasma.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Zellweger (no peroxisomes), X-ALD (VLCFA, adrenal + demyelination), Refsum (phytanic acid).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Ragged-red fibers + maternal inheritance + lactic acidosis = mitochondrial disease.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Vincristine/vinblastine block assembly; paclitaxel blocks disassembly; colchicine for gout.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Kartagener: dynein arm defect, situs inversus, infertility, bronchiectasis.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Cytokeratin carcinoma, vimentin sarcoma, desmin muscle, GFAP glia.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Intrinsic = cytochrome c / caspase 9; extrinsic = Fas / caspase 8; BCL-2 t(14;18) follicular lymphoma; annexin V detects PS.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">p53 → p21 → G1 arrest; Rb holds E2F; HPV E6 (p53), E7 (Rb).</li>\n<li>COPII forward (ER → Golgi), COPI backward, clathrin to lysosomes/endocytosis.</li>\n<li>Cholesterol buffers fluidity; PS inner leaflet; cholera toxin binds GM1.</li>\n</ul>", "fbs-biochem": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Carbohydrates</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Classified by carbon number (triose, pentose, hexose) and carbonyl: <b>aldose</b> (aldehyde: glucose, galactose, ribose) vs <b>ketose</b> (ketone: fructose).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">D/L = position of –OH on the chiral carbon farthest from the carbonyl</span> (right = D). Natural sugars are mostly D; natural amino acids are L.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Epimers differ at ONE carbon: glucose/galactose (C4), glucose/mannose (C2).</span> Enantiomers = mirror images. Anomers = differ at the anomeric carbon (α vs β) after ring closure; interconvert by mutarotation.</li>\n<li><b>Glycosidic bonds</b>: O-glycosidic (sugar–OH; disaccharides) vs N-glycosidic (sugar–N; nucleosides, N-linked glycoproteins).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Reducing sugar = free anomeric carbon</span> (Benedict’s/Fehling’s positive): glucose, fructose, galactose, maltose, lactose. <span class=\"hy\">Sucrose is NON-reducing</span> (both anomeric carbons used). Trehalose also non-reducing.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Disaccharide</th><th>Units &amp; bond</th><th>Enzyme</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Sucrose</td><td>Glucose α1→β2 fructose (non-reducing)</td><td>Sucrase</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Lactose</td><td>Galactose β1→4 glucose</td><td>Lactase (β-galactosidase) — deficiency → bloating, diarrhea, + hydrogen breath test</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Maltose</td><td>Glucose α1→4 glucose</td><td>Maltase</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trehalose</td><td>Glucose α1→1 glucose</td><td>Trehalase</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Polysaccharides</b>: <span class=\"hy\">glycogen &amp; amylopectin branch with α(1→6)</span> on α(1→4) chains (glycogen every 8–10 residues — more branched); amylose = unbranched α(1→4); <span class=\"hy\">cellulose = β(1→4), humans can’t digest (fiber)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Liver glycogen maintains blood glucose (has glucose-6-phosphatase); muscle glycogen fuels muscle only.</span> Liver stores last ~12–24 h of fasting.</li>\n<li><b>GAGs</b>: long unbranched repeating disaccharides (amino sugar + uronic acid), highly <b>negatively charged</b> (sulfate, carboxyl) → bind Na⁺ and water → gels that cushion/lubricate. Bound to core proteins as <b>proteoglycans</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>GAG</th><th>Key location / fact</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Hyaluronic acid</td><td>Only non-sulfated GAG, not attached to core protein; synovial fluid, vitreous, cartilage aggregates</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Heparin</td><td>Only intracellular GAG (mast cell granules); most negatively charged; activates antithrombin</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chondroitin sulfate</td><td>Most abundant; cartilage (aggrecan), bone</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Keratan sulfate</td><td>Cornea, cartilage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dermatan sulfate</td><td>Skin, vessels, heart valves</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Heparan sulfate</td><td>Basement membranes, cell surfaces (glomerular charge barrier)</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Glycosylation (Anki)</h2>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>N-linked</th><th>O-linked</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Residue</td><td class=\"hy\">Asparagine (amide N), consensus Asn-X-Ser/Thr (X ≠ Pro)</td><td class=\"hy\">Serine/threonine –OH (hydroxylysine in collagen); no strict consensus</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Begins co-translationally in ER, modified in Golgi</td><td>Golgi</td></tr>\n<tr><td>How</td><td class=\"hy\">En bloc: 14-sugar Glc₃Man₉GlcNAc₂ built on dolichol phosphate, transferred by oligosaccharyltransferase (OST)</td><td>One sugar at a time by glycosyltransferases (mucin-type starts with GalNAc)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Quality control</b>: <span class=\"hy\">calnexin/calreticulin</span> retain misfolded glycoproteins in the ER (glucose trimming/re-adding); persistent misfolding → ERAD → ubiquitin–proteasome.</li>\n<li><b>Tunicamycin</b> blocks dolichol-linked oligosaccharide synthesis → no N-glycosylation.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Congenital disorders of glycosylation</span>: multisystem — hypotonia, developmental delay, abnormal fat pads, inverted nipples, coagulopathy; screen with transferrin isoforms.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Amino acids</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>All proteins use <span class=\"hy\">L-amino acids</span>; <span class=\"hy\">glycine is the only achiral amino acid</span> (R = H; smallest, flexible; every 3rd residue in collagen). D-amino acids in bacterial walls (D-Ala-D-Ala, vancomycin target).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Proline</span>: R group bonds its own amino N (imino acid) → rigid, no backbone H-bond donor → <span class=\"hy\">α-helix breaker</span>, found at turns; hydroxyproline in collagen. Cis peptide bonds mainly occur before proline.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Essential amino acids: PVT TIM HALL</span> — Phe, Val, Thr, Trp, Ile, Met, His, Arg (conditionally), Leu, Lys.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Purely ketogenic: Leucine and Lysine</span>. Both glucogenic + ketogenic: Phe, Tyr, Trp, Ile, Thr. Ketogenic → acetyl-CoA/acetoacetate; cannot make net glucose (PDH irreversible).</li>\n<li><b>Aromatic</b> (Phe, Tyr, Trp) <span class=\"hy\">absorb UV at 280 nm</span> (protein assay); nucleic acids at 260 nm; NADH 340 nm.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cysteine</span> –SH forms <b>disulfide bonds</b> (secreted proteins, insulin, antibodies); part of glutathione. Met &amp; Cys contain sulfur.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Histidine</span>: imidazole pKa ≈ 6 (near physiologic pH) → buffering; <b>proximal His</b> binds heme Fe²⁺, <b>distal His</b> stabilizes O₂ and sterically limits CO binding (CO affinity ~200–250× O₂). Bohr effect: H⁺ protonates His → T state → O₂ unloading.</li>\n<li>Acidic: Asp, Glu (negative at pH 7). Basic: Lys, Arg (positive), His (partly). Polar uncharged: Ser, Thr, Asn, Gln, Cys, Tyr.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Sickle cell: Glu → Val at β6 (HbS)</span>; deoxy-HbS polymerizes (hydrophobic Val). HbC: Glu → Lys (milder, target cells).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Acid–base properties &amp; pI</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Henderson–Hasselbalch: pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])</span>. At pH = pKa → 50:50; best buffering within ±1 pH unit of pKa.</li>\n<li>Zwitterion at physiologic pH (–NH₃⁺, –COO⁻). <span class=\"hy\">pI = pH with net charge 0</span>: neutral AA = average of the two pKas (~6); acidic AA = average of the two acidic pKas (low pI); basic AA = average of the two basic pKas (high pI).</li>\n<li>pH &lt; pI → net positive (moves to cathode). pH &gt; pI → net negative (moves to anode). Proteins are least soluble at their pI (precipitate).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Proteins: structure, folding &amp; misfolding</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Level</th><th>Defined by</th><th>Bonds</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Primary</td><td>Amino-acid sequence (N → C)</td><td class=\"hy\">Peptide (covalent amide) bonds — planar, rigid, partial double-bond, usually trans</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Secondary</td><td>α-helix, β-sheet, turns</td><td class=\"hy\">Backbone H-bonds (C=O to N–H)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tertiary</td><td>3D fold of one chain (domains)</td><td>Hydrophobic interactions (main), ionic, H-bonds, disulfides</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Quaternary</td><td>Multiple subunits (e.g., Hb α₂β₂)</td><td>Same non-covalent ± disulfides</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li>Insulin: two chains, <b>3 disulfides</b> (2 interchain, 1 intrachain on A chain).</li>\n<li>Classification: fibrous (collagen, keratin, elastin — structural, insoluble) vs globular (enzymes, Hb, antibodies — soluble); simple vs conjugated (glyco-, lipo-, metallo-, hemoproteins).</li>\n<li><b>Denaturation</b> (heat, pH, urea, detergents, heavy metals) disrupts 2°–4° structure; primary intact. <b>Anfinsen</b>: sequence determines fold (ribonuclease refolds).</li>\n<li><b>Chaperones</b> (Hsp70, Hsp90, chaperonins Hsp60) assist folding; misfolded proteins → ubiquitin → proteasome.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Misfolding diseases</span>: amyloidosis (β-pleated sheet, Congo red → apple-green birefringence), <span class=\"hy\">Alzheimer (Aβ plaques, tau tangles)</span>, <span class=\"hy\">prion diseases (PrPᶜ α-helix → PrPˢᶜ β-sheet; CJD, kuru, mad cow; spongiform, protease-resistant)</span>, Huntington, Parkinson (α-synuclein), cystic fibrosis ΔF508 (misfolded CFTR degraded), α₁-antitrypsin deficiency (PiZ retained in hepatocyte ER → cirrhosis + emphysema).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Enzymes (Anki)</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Class</th><th>Reaction</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1 Oxidoreductases</td><td>Electron transfer (dehydrogenases, oxidases)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2 Transferases</td><td>Move a group (kinases transfer phosphate, transaminases)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>3 Hydrolases</td><td>Break bonds by adding water (proteases, lipases, phosphatases)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4 Lyases</td><td>Break bonds without water/oxidation (decarboxylases, aldolase)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5 Isomerases</td><td>Rearrange within a molecule (mutases, epimerases)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>6 Ligases</td><td>Join two molecules using ATP (synthetases, carboxylases)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Km = [S] at ½ Vmax (inverse of affinity); Vmax ∝ enzyme amount.</span> Lineweaver–Burk: x-intercept = −1/Km, y-intercept = 1/Vmax.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Inhibitor</th><th>Binds</th><th>Km</th><th>Vmax</th><th>L–B plot</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Competitive (reversible)</td><td>Active site (free E)</td><td>↑</td><td>—</td><td>Lines cross on y-axis; overcome by ↑ [S]</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Noncompetitive</td><td>Allosteric site (E and ES)</td><td>—</td><td>↓</td><td>Lines cross on x-axis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Uncompetitive</td><td>ES complex only</td><td>↓</td><td>↓</td><td>Parallel lines</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Irreversible</td><td>Covalent (aspirin–COX, organophosphates)</td><td>—</td><td>↓</td><td>Like noncompetitive</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><b>Feedback inhibition</b>: end product inhibits an early enzyme. <b>Feedforward activation</b>: early metabolite activates a later enzyme (F-1,6-BP → pyruvate kinase).</li></ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Vitamins (water-soluble)</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Vitamin</th><th>Coenzyme</th><th>Function / key enzymes</th><th>Deficiency</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>B1 Thiamine</td><td>TPP</td><td>Oxidative decarboxylation &amp; transketolase: PDH, α-KG DH, BCKDH, transketolase <span class=\"mn\">(Be APT)</span></td><td>Wet beriberi (high-output HF), dry beriberi (neuropathy), Wernicke (confusion, ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus, ataxia), Korsakoff (amnesia, confabulation)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>B2 Riboflavin</td><td>FAD, FMN</td><td>Redox; succinate DH (Complex II)</td><td>Cheilosis, magenta tongue, corneal vascularization <span class=\"mn\">(2 C’s of B2)</span></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>B3 Niacin</td><td>NAD⁺, NADP⁺</td><td>Redox; NADPH for reductive biosynthesis; made from Trp (needs B2, B6)</td><td>Pellagra: Dermatitis (Casal necklace), Diarrhea, Dementia (± Death)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>B5 Pantothenate</td><td>CoA</td><td>Acyl transfer; fatty acid synthase</td><td>Rare: dermatitis, alopecia, adrenal insufficiency, burning feet</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>B6 Pyridoxine</td><td>PLP</td><td>Transamination (ALT, AST), decarboxylation, ALA synthase (heme), glutamate → GABA, glycogen phosphorylase</td><td>Convulsions, peripheral neuropathy, sideroblastic anemia, cheilosis</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>B7 Biotin</td><td>Biotin itself</td><td>Carboxylation: pyruvate carboxylase, acetyl-CoA carboxylase, propionyl-CoA carboxylase</td><td>Dermatitis, alopecia, enteritis, lactic acidosis</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>B9 Folate</td><td>THF</td><td>One-carbon transfer: purines, thymidine (dTMP)</td><td>Megaloblastic anemia, hypersegmented PMNs, ↑ homocysteine, <b>normal MMA</b>, <b>no neuro signs</b>; neural tube defects</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>B12 Cobalamin</td><td>Methyl- &amp; adenosyl-cobalamin</td><td>Methionine synthase; methylmalonyl-CoA mutase</td><td>Megaloblastic anemia + ↑ homocysteine + <b>↑ MMA</b> + subacute combined degeneration (dorsal columns, lateral corticospinal, spinocerebellar)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>C Ascorbate</td><td>—</td><td>Hydroxylation of Pro/Lys (collagen), dopamine β-hydroxylase, Fe³⁺→Fe²⁺ absorption, antioxidant</td><td>Scurvy: bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage, corkscrew hairs, poor wound healing</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Thiamine before glucose</span> in alcoholics (glucose metabolism consumes TPP → precipitates Wernicke). Dx thiamine: ↑ RBC transketolase activity after adding TPP. Brain &amp; heart (aerobic) suffer first.</li>\n<li>B1 causes: alcoholism (most common US), malnutrition, hyperemesis, bariatric surgery.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Pellagra causes beyond diet: Hartnup disease (↓ Trp absorption), carcinoid syndrome (Trp → serotonin), isoniazid (↓ B6).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Niacin drug</span>: best ↑ HDL, ↓ LDL/TG; side effects flushing (prostaglandin; pretreat with aspirin), hyperglycemia, hyperuricemia (gout).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Isoniazid → B6 deficiency → neuropathy; give pyridoxine.</span> Also penicillamine, OCPs.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Biotin: raw egg whites (avidin) or long antibiotics.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Folate deficiency causes: alcoholism, pregnancy, methotrexate, trimethoprim, phenytoin.</span> Stores last <b>months</b>; give before conception (0.4 mg; 4 mg if prior NTD).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">B12 causes: pernicious anemia (anti-IF/parietal cell antibodies), gastrectomy, terminal ileum disease/resection (Crohn), Diphyllobothrium latum, vegan diet, metformin.</span> Absorbed with intrinsic factor (parietal cells) in the <b>terminal ileum</b>; stores last <b>years</b>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Giving folate alone in B12 deficiency corrects the anemia but NOT the neurologic damage.</span></li>\n<li>Humans can’t make vitamin C (lack L-gulonolactone oxidase). Fat-soluble A, D, E, K are stored (toxicity possible) and need fat absorption.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Redox vitamins = B2 + B3; decarboxylation = B1 + B6; carboxylation = biotin; one-carbon = folate (+B12); acyl transfer = B5; hydroxylation = vitamin C.</span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Rapid-fire NBME pearls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">Glucose vs galactose = C4 epimers; sucrose non-reducing; glycogen branches α(1→6).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Hyaluronic acid only non-sulfated; heparin only intracellular GAG.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">N-linked = Asn-X-Ser/Thr (ER, dolichol, en bloc); O-linked = Ser/Thr (Golgi, one at a time).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Proline breaks helices; glycine achiral; leucine &amp; lysine purely ketogenic; aromatics absorb 280 nm.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Competitive: Km ↑, Vmax same. Noncompetitive: Vmax ↓, Km same.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Wernicke triad + alcoholic → thiamine (before glucose). ↑ MMA → B12, not folate.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Prions = PrP α→β conversion; amyloid stains Congo red (apple-green).</li>\n</ul>", "fbs-embryology": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Meiosis &amp; chromosome counting</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Cell</th><th>Ploidy (n)</th><th>DNA (c)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Spermatogonium / oogonium (before S)</td><td>2n</td><td>2c</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Primary spermatocyte / oocyte (after S)</td><td>2n (46)</td><td>4c</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Secondary spermatocyte / oocyte (after meiosis I)</td><td>1n (23)</td><td>2c</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Spermatid / ovum (after meiosis II)</td><td>1n</td><td>1c</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Meiosis I = reductional (homologs separate; crossing over in prophase I). Meiosis II = equational (sister chromatids separate).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Nondisjunction (mostly maternal meiosis I) ↑ with maternal age → trisomy 21, 18, 13; Klinefelter (47,XXY), Turner (45,X).</span></li>\n<li>Primordial germ cells arise in the <b>yolk sac wall (epiblast origin)</b> and migrate to the gonadal ridge by week 5–6; lost PGCs → germ cell tumors (sacrococcygeal teratoma).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Spermatogenesis &amp; the male tract</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Begins at puberty, in seminiferous tubules; ~64–74 days. Spermatogonia (mitosis) → primary spermatocyte → secondary spermatocyte → spermatids → <b>spermiogenesis</b> (shape change only, no division).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Spermiogenesis: acrosome from Golgi (hyaluronidase, acrosin), flagellum from centriole, mitochondria form midpiece sheath, cytoplasm shed.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Sertoli cells (FSH)</span>: blood–testis barrier (tight junctions), androgen-binding protein, <b>inhibin B</b> (→ ↓ FSH), <b>Müllerian-inhibiting factor/AMH</b> (Müllerian ducts regress), support spermatogenesis. <span class=\"hy\">Leydig cells (LH)</span>: interstitial, make <b>testosterone</b> <span class=\"mn\">(L for LH, L for Leydig)</span>.</li>\n<li>Sperm path: <span class=\"mn\">SEVEN UP</span> — Seminiferous tubules → (rete testis, efferent ductules) → Epididymis (maturation, motility, storage) → Vas deferens → Ejaculatory duct → (Nothing) → Urethra → Penis.</li>\n<li><b>Capacitation</b> (in female tract; removal of cholesterol/glycoproteins) → <b>acrosome reaction</b> on binding ZP3 of the zona pellucida.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Oogenesis &amp; the ovary</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Oogonia multiply only before birth (months 3–7)</span>; primary oocytes <span class=\"hy\">arrest in prophase I (diplotene) until puberty</span>; most undergo <b>atresia</b> (~7 million fetal → 1–2 million at birth → ~400,000 at puberty → ~400 ovulated).</li>\n<li>Each cycle: LH surge → meiosis I completes → secondary oocyte + 1st polar body → <span class=\"hy\">secondary oocyte arrested in metaphase II until fertilization</span> <span class=\"mn\">(“Ovulated, waiting to Meet a sperm”)</span>.</li>\n<li>Follicle stages: primordial (flat cells) → <b>primary</b> (single cuboidal layer; zona pellucida forms) → secondary (2+ layers, antrum begins) → tertiary/<b>Graafian</b> (large antrum, cumulus oophorus).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Two-cell model: theca cells (LH) make androgens → diffuse to granulosa cells (FSH) → aromatase → estrogen.</span> <i>Your deck:</i> theca interna = vascular, hormone-secreting layer (estrogen); theca externa = fibrous.</li>\n<li>Granulosa = female counterpart of Sertoli; theca = counterpart of Leydig.</li>\n<li><b>Corpus luteum</b> (from ruptured follicle) → <span class=\"hy\">progesterone</span> (+ estrogen). Degenerates after ~14 days → corpus albicans, unless <span class=\"hy\">hCG</span> from the syncytiotrophoblast rescues it (until placenta takes over ~weeks 8–10).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Menstrual cycle &amp; hormones</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Days</th><th>Ovary</th><th>Endometrium</th><th>Hormones</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Menstrual</td><td>1–5</td><td>Early follicle recruitment</td><td>Functional layer shed</td><td>↓ estrogen &amp; progesterone</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Follicular / proliferative</td><td>~6–14</td><td>Dominant follicle grows</td><td>Proliferates (estrogen)</td><td>FSH ↑ early, estrogen rises</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Ovulation</td><td>~14</td><td>Graafian follicle ruptures 24–36 h after LH surge</td><td>—</td><td>Estrogen peak → positive feedback → <b>LH surge</b></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Luteal / secretory</td><td>15–28 (fixed 14 days)</td><td>Corpus luteum</td><td>Glands secrete glycogen; receptive</td><td>Progesterone ↑ (basal body temp ↑ ~0.5 °F)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Variation in cycle length comes from the follicular phase; luteal phase is fixed ~14 days.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Menses occurs because the corpus luteum degenerates → progesterone falls → spiral arteries constrict → ischemia → shedding.</span></li>\n<li>Estrogen: endometrial proliferation, thin stretchy cervical mucus (spinnbarkeit). Progesterone: secretory endometrium, thick mucus, maintains pregnancy, ↑ temperature.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Combined OCPs</span>: negative feedback ↓ FSH/LH → no follicle maturation, no LH surge (no ovulation); thick cervical mucus; thin endometrium.</li>\n<li>Endometrium layers: functional (shed) vs basal (regenerates). <b>Decidua</b> = pregnancy endometrium: basalis (maternal placenta), capsularis, parietalis.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Fertilization, cleavage &amp; implantation (week 1)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Fertilization in the ampulla of the uterine tube, within ~24 h of ovulation.</span></li>\n<li>Sperm penetrate corona radiata → <b>zona pellucida</b> (ZP3 binding, acrosome reaction) → fusion. <span class=\"hy\">Cortical reaction / zona reaction blocks polyspermy</span> (Ca²⁺ wave; ZP changes). Polyspermy → triploidy (partial mole 69,XXY).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Results of fertilization: meiosis II completed, diploidy restored, sex determined (X or Y sperm), cleavage initiated.</span></li>\n<li>Cleavage: mitosis without growth → blastomeres → <span class=\"hy\">morula (16 cells, ~day 3)</span> → enters uterus → <span class=\"hy\">blastocyst (~day 4–5)</span>: outer cells pump Na⁺ in, water follows → blastocyst cavity. <b>Trophoblast</b> (→ placenta) + <b>inner cell mass/embryoblast</b> (→ embryo).</li>\n<li>Hatches from zona → <span class=\"hy\">implantation begins ~day 6</span> in the posterior-superior uterine wall (endometrium in secretory/luteal phase). hCG detectable in blood ~day 8, urine ~day 10–14.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Ectopic pregnancy</span>: most often <b>ampulla</b>; risk ↑ with PID/salpingitis, prior ectopic, surgery, IUD, Kartagener. Abdominal pain + vaginal bleeding + ↑ hCG, empty uterus on US. Placenta previa = implantation near/over the internal os (painless bleeding).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Week 2: “week of twos”</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">2 layers of trophoblast: cytotrophoblast (inner, mitotic) + syncytiotrophoblast (outer, invasive, makes hCG).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">2 layers of embryoblast (bilaminar disc): epiblast (→ amniotic cavity; later gives all 3 germ layers) + hypoblast (→ primary yolk sac).</span> <i>Your deck</i> pairs epiblast ≈ ectoderm, hypoblast ≈ endoderm.</li>\n<li>2 cavities: amniotic cavity &amp; yolk sac (+ chorionic cavity from splitting of extraembryonic mesoderm). Connecting stalk → umbilical cord.</li>\n<li><b>Villi</b>: primary (cytotrophoblast core + syncytio) → secondary (+ extraembryonic mesoderm core) → <span class=\"hy\">tertiary (+ fetal blood vessels, end of week 3)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">hCG</span> (α subunit like LH/FSH/TSH) maintains the corpus luteum; very high in moles/choriocarcinoma (can cause hyperthyroidism, hyperemesis).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Hydatidiform mole</span>: complete = 46,XX (empty egg + paternal duplication), no fetus, “snowstorm”/grape-like, very ↑ hCG, ↑ choriocarcinoma risk. Partial = 69,XXY (2 sperm), fetal parts present, lower risk.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Yolk sac, primitive gut &amp; umbilical cord</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Yolk sac: early blood formation (blood islands, week 3), primordial germ cells, contributes to primitive gut (folding incorporates it).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Vitelline duct normally obliterates; persistence → Meckel diverticulum</span> (rule of 2s: 2% of people, 2 feet from ileocecal valve, 2 inches long, 2 types of ectopic tissue — gastric/pancreatic, presents by age 2; painless bleeding) or vitelline fistula (meconium from umbilicus).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Umbilical cord: 2 arteries (deoxygenated blood to placenta) + 1 vein (oxygenated to fetus), Wharton jelly.</span> Single umbilical artery → associated with renal and cardiac anomalies.</li>\n<li><b>Allantois</b> → urachus (median umbilical ligament); patent urachus → urine from umbilicus.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g8\">8. Week 3: gastrulation &amp; germ layers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Gastrulation = formation of 3 germ layers from epiblast via the primitive streak (begins with primitive node/streak at the caudal end).</span> Epiblast cells invaginate: first replace hypoblast → <b>endoderm</b>; then between → <b>mesoderm</b>; remaining epiblast → <b>ectoderm</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Notochord</b> (from primitive node) → induces neural plate; adult remnant = <span class=\"hy\">nucleus pulposus</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Sacrococcygeal teratoma</span> = remnant of primitive streak (most common tumor in newborns). <span class=\"hy\">Caudal regression syndrome</span> (sirenomelia) — maternal diabetes.</li>\n<li>Oropharyngeal (buccopharyngeal) and cloacal membranes: ectoderm + endoderm, no mesoderm.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Week 3–8 = embryonic period: most susceptible to teratogens (organogenesis).</span> Weeks 1–2: “all-or-none”.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Germ layer</th><th>Gives rise to</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Surface ectoderm</td><td>Epidermis, hair, nails, lens, anterior pituitary (Rathke pouch), enamel, mammary/sweat glands, inner ear</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Neuroectoderm</td><td>Brain, spinal cord, retina, posterior pituitary, pineal, oligodendrocytes, astrocytes</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Neural crest</td><td>PNS ganglia, Schwann cells, melanocytes, adrenal medulla (chromaffin), odontoblasts, parafollicular C cells, pia/arachnoid, aorticopulmonary septum, craniofacial bones <span class=\"mn\">(MOTEL PASS)</span></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Mesoderm</td><td>Muscle, bone, connective tissue, heart, blood/lymph vessels, kidneys, gonads, dermis, spleen, adrenal cortex, serous linings</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Endoderm</td><td>Gut epithelium, liver, pancreas, lungs, thyroid follicular cells, thymus, parathyroids, bladder/urethra epithelium</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g9\">9. Mesoderm, body cavities &amp; neurulation</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Mesoderm divisions: <b>paraxial</b> → somites (sclerotome = vertebrae/ribs; myotome = skeletal muscle; dermatome = dermis); <b>intermediate</b> → urogenital system (kidneys, gonads); <b>lateral plate</b> → somatic (parietal) layer (body wall, limb bones/CT, parietal serosa) &amp; splanchnic (visceral) layer (gut wall muscle, heart, visceral serosa).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Intraembryonic coelom</span> (space within lateral plate) → pericardial, pleural and peritoneal cavities; septum transversum → central tendon of diaphragm, part of liver.</li>\n<li><b>Neurulation (weeks 3–4)</b>: notochord induces neural plate → neural folds → neural tube; <span class=\"hy\">anterior neuropore closes ~day 25, posterior ~day 27–28</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Neural tube defects</span>: anencephaly (anterior neuropore; polyhydramnios — can’t swallow), spina bifida occulta (no herniation, tuft of hair/dimple), meningocele, myelomeningocele (posterior neuropore). <span class=\"hy\">↑ AFP in maternal serum &amp; amniotic fluid, ↑ AChE in amniotic fluid</span>. Prevent: <b>folic acid</b>. Risk: valproate, carbamazepine, maternal diabetes, folate deficiency.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g10\">10. Placenta: structure, variants &amp; pathology</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Fetal part = <b>chorion frondosum</b> (villous chorion); maternal part = <b>decidua basalis</b>. Exchange across the <b>placental membrane</b> (syncytiotrophoblast, cytotrophoblast — thins later — villous connective tissue, fetal endothelium). Maternal blood fills intervillous spaces from spiral arteries.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Syncytiotrophoblast makes hCG, hPL (human placental lactogen → insulin resistance), estrogen (estriol), progesterone.</span></li>\n<li>Crosses: O₂, CO₂, glucose, <span class=\"hy\">IgG (only immunoglobulin)</span>, lipid-soluble drugs, alcohol, many viruses (TORCH), anti-D antibodies (Rh disease). Does not cross: IgM, heparin, insulin (largely).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Placenta accreta / increta / percreta</span>: defective decidua basalis → villi attach to / invade / penetrate myometrium; risk prior C-section; postpartum hemorrhage.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Placental abruption</span>: premature separation → <b>painful</b> bleeding (cocaine, smoking, HTN, trauma); <b>Placenta previa</b> → <b>painless</b> bleeding.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Preeclampsia</span>: abnormal spiral artery remodeling → HTN + proteinuria after 20 weeks; eclampsia = + seizures (Mg sulfate); HELLP.</li>\n<li>Variants: succenturiate (accessory) lobe, velamentous cord insertion (vasa previa risk), battledore (marginal), circumvallate.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g11\">11. Prenatal testing, amniotic fluid &amp; twins</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Amniotic fluid</span>: mostly fetal urine after ~week 16; removed by fetal swallowing. Normal ~1 L at term.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Polyhydramnios (&gt;2 L)</span>: can’t swallow — esophageal/duodenal atresia, anencephaly; maternal diabetes, twins. <span class=\"hy\">Oligohydramnios (&lt;0.5 L)</span>: can’t make/excrete urine — bilateral renal agenesis, posterior urethral valves, placental insufficiency, ROM → <b>Potter sequence</b> (pulmonary hypoplasia, flat face, limb deformities, clubfoot).</li>\n<li><b>Screening</b>: quad screen (~15–20 weeks): AFP, hCG, estriol, inhibin A. <span class=\"hy\">Down syndrome: ↓ AFP, ↑ hCG, ↓ estriol, ↑ inhibin A</span>. Trisomy 18: all ↓ (inhibin normal). NTD/abdominal wall defect/twins: ↑ AFP. First-trimester: ↑ nuchal translucency, ↓ PAPP-A, ↑ β-hCG (Down). Cell-free fetal DNA from ~10 weeks.</li>\n<li><b>Diagnostic</b>: <span class=\"hy\">chorionic villus sampling (~10–13 weeks)</span>; <span class=\"hy\">amniocentesis (~15–20 weeks)</span>; percutaneous umbilical blood sampling (cordocentesis). Lecithin/sphingomyelin ratio &gt;2 = fetal lung maturity.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Twins</span>: dizygotic (fraternal, 2/3; two eggs) → always dichorionic diamniotic. <span class=\"hy\">Monozygotic (identical) — timing of split decides membranes</span>:</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Split (days)</th><th>Stage</th><th>Result</th></tr>\n<tr><td>0–4</td><td>Morula (2-cell to morula)</td><td>Dichorionic, diamniotic (2 placentas)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>4–8</td><td>Blastocyst (inner cell mass splits)</td><td>Monochorionic, diamniotic (most common MZ) — twin–twin transfusion risk</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8–12</td><td>Bilaminar disc</td><td>Monochorionic, monoamniotic (cord entanglement)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>&gt;13</td><td>Embryonic disc</td><td>Conjoined twins</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g12\">12. Rapid-fire NBME pearls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">Primary oocyte: prophase I arrest until puberty. Secondary oocyte: metaphase II arrest until fertilization.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">LH → Leydig (testosterone); FSH → Sertoli (ABP, inhibin, AMH). Theca androgens → granulosa estrogen.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Fertilization in ampulla; implantation day 6; ectopic usually ampulla.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Week 2 = 2s (cyto/syncytio; epi/hypoblast; amnion/yolk sac). Week 3 = 3 germ layers (gastrulation), notochord → nucleus pulposus.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">NTD: ↑ AFP + ↑ AChE; prevent with folate. Down: ↓ AFP, ↑ hCG, ↓ estriol, ↑ inhibin.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Complete mole 46,XX paternal, no fetus; partial 69,XXY with fetal parts.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Monochorionic diamniotic = split days 4–8 (most common MZ twins).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Oligohydramnios → Potter sequence (renal agenesis); polyhydramnios → GI atresia/anencephaly.</li>\n</ul>", "msk-histology": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Muscle</h2>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Skeletal</th><th>Cardiac</th><th>Smooth</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Nuclei</td><td class=\"hy\">Many, peripheral</td><td class=\"hy\">1–2, central</td><td>1, central (cigar/corkscrew when contracted)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Striations</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Special</td><td>Triads (T-tubule at <b>A–I junction</b> + 2 terminal cisternae)</td><td class=\"hy\">Intercalated discs (fascia adherens, desmosomes, gap junctions); dyads at <b>Z-line</b>; branched</td><td>Dense bodies (α-actinin), caveolae, gap junctions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Regeneration</td><td>Satellite cells (limited)</td><td class=\"hy\">None → fibrous scar</td><td>Good (mitosis, pericytes)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ca²⁺ binds</td><td>Troponin C</td><td>Troponin C</td><td class=\"hy\">Calmodulin → MLCK</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li>Organization: epimysium (whole muscle) → perimysium (fascicles) → <b>endomysium</b> (each fiber; reticular fibers) → fiber → myofibrils → sarcomeres.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Sarcomere = Z to Z line. A band (myosin, entire length) stays constant. I band (actin only), H zone (myosin only) shorten during contraction. M line = middle of H zone.</span> <span class=\"mn\">(HIZ shrinkage; A is Always the same)</span></li>\n<li>Thin filament = actin + tropomyosin + troponin (T binds tropomyosin, I inhibits, C binds Ca²⁺). Thick = myosin II.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Accessory proteins</span>: <b>titin</b> (Z to M; elasticity, centers myosin; largest protein), <b>nebulin</b> (molecular ruler for actin length), <b>α-actinin</b> (anchors actin at Z), <b>desmin</b> (links Z discs to each other and sarcolemma), <b>dystrophin</b> (actin → dystroglycan → laminin; Duchenne/Becker), myomesin (M line), CapZ (+ end), tropomodulin (− end).</li>\n<li>Fiber types: <span class=\"hy\">type I = slow oxidative, red (myoglobin, mitochondria), posture, marathon</span> <span class=\"mn\">(“one slow red ox”)</span>; type IIa fast oxidative-glycolytic; <b>type IIb/x = fast glycolytic, white, sprint</b>.</li>\n<li>Muscle spindle (intrafusal: nuclear bag/chain; γ motor; senses length) vs Golgi tendon organ (tendon; senses tension; Ib).</li>\n<li><b>Satellite cells</b>: muscle stem cells under the basal lamina; hypertrophy (not hyperplasia) after exercise.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Cell junctions &amp; cytoskeleton</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Junction</th><th>Proteins</th><th>Cytoskeleton</th><th>Function / disease</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Tight (zonula occludens)</td><td>Claudins, occludin</td><td>Actin</td><td>Seal paracellular space; apical/basolateral polarity; C. perfringens toxin targets claudins</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Adherens (zonula adherens)</td><td>E-cadherin (Ca²⁺-dependent), catenins</td><td>Actin</td><td>Belt; loss of E-cadherin → metastasis, diffuse gastric/lobular breast cancer</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Desmosome (macula adherens)</td><td>Desmogleins, desmocollins (cadherins), plakoglobin, desmoplakin</td><td>Intermediate (keratin)</td><td>Spot weld; <b>pemphigus vulgaris</b> = anti-desmoglein 1/3 → intraepidermal, flaccid bullae, + Nikolsky</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gap junction</td><td>Connexins (connexon)</td><td>—</td><td>Electrical/metabolic coupling (cardiac, smooth muscle); Charcot–Marie–Tooth X-linked (connexin 32)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Hemidesmosome</td><td>Integrins (α6β4), BP180/BP230</td><td>Intermediate</td><td>Cell → basement membrane; <b>bullous pemphigoid</b> = anti-hemidesmosome → subepidermal tense bullae, Nikolsky −</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Focal adhesion</td><td>Integrins → fibronectin</td><td>Actin</td><td>Cell–ECM; migration signaling</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Intermediate filaments = tumor markers: cytokeratin (epithelium/carcinoma), vimentin (mesenchyme/sarcoma), desmin (muscle), GFAP (glia), neurofilament (neurons), lamins (nucleus).</span></li>\n<li>Microvilli = actin core (absorptive surface); stereocilia = long actin microvilli (epididymis, inner ear hair cells); cilia = microtubules 9+2 with dynein (Kartagener).</li>\n<li>Basement membrane = basal lamina (type IV collagen, laminin, heparan sulfate, nidogen) + reticular lamina (type III).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Cartilage</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Collagen</th><th>Perichondrium</th><th>Where</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Hyaline</td><td>Type II</td><td>Yes (except articular)</td><td>Articular surfaces, costal, trachea, larynx (most), nose, <b>fetal skeleton &amp; epiphyseal plate</b></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Elastic</td><td>Type II + elastic fibers</td><td>Yes</td><td>External ear, epiglottis, auditory tube</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Fibrocartilage</td><td><b>Type I</b> + II</td><td><b>No</b></td><td>Intervertebral disc (annulus), pubic symphysis, menisci, TMJ, tendon insertions</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cartilage is avascular</span> (nutrients diffuse from perichondrium/synovial fluid) → poor healing.</li>\n<li>Chondrocytes in lacunae; <b>isogenous groups</b> (from one cell). Matrix: aggrecan (chondroitin + keratan sulfate on hyaluronan) → water binding → compression resistance.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Growth: appositional (from perichondrium chondroblasts) vs interstitial (chondrocytes dividing inside; growth plate).</span></li>\n<li>Osteoarthritis = articular cartilage degeneration (no perichondrium to repair).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Bone</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Matrix: <span class=\"hy\">type I collagen</span> (osteoid) + hydroxyapatite. Cells:\n<ul><li><b>Osteoprogenitor</b> (mesenchymal, RUNX2) → <span class=\"hy\">osteoblasts</span> (cuboidal, on surfaces; make osteoid, <b>alkaline phosphatase</b>, osteocalcin; express <b>RANKL</b> and OPG; PTH receptors).</li>\n<li><b>Osteocytes</b> (trapped in lacunae, connected by canaliculi via gap junctions; mechanosensors; sclerostin).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Osteoclasts</span>: multinucleated, from <b>monocyte/macrophage lineage</b>; ruffled border + sealing zone (integrins) form <b>Howship lacuna</b>; secrete H⁺ (carbonic anhydrase II) and cathepsin K; <b>tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase (TRAP)</b>. Activated by <span class=\"hy\">RANKL–RANK</span> + M-CSF; OPG = decoy receptor; denosumab = anti-RANKL. Calcitonin inhibits.</li></ul></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">PTH acts on osteoblasts (receptors) → ↑ RANKL → osteoclast activation.</span> Intermittent PTH (teriparatide) builds bone.</li>\n<li><b>Compact (cortical) bone</b>: osteons/Haversian systems — concentric lamellae around a Haversian canal; <b>Volkmann canals</b> run perpendicular, connecting them; interstitial and circumferential lamellae. <b>Spongy (cancellous)</b>: trabeculae, red marrow, no osteons.</li>\n<li>Woven (primary, immature; random collagen; fracture callus, Paget) vs lamellar (mature).</li>\n<li>Periosteum (outer fibrous + inner osteogenic layer; Sharpey fibers) vs endosteum.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Ossification: intramembranous (flat skull bones, clavicle, mandible — mesenchyme → bone directly) vs endochondral (long bones, vertebrae, base of skull — hyaline cartilage model replaced).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Growth plate zones (epiphysis → diaphysis): reserve (resting) → proliferation (stacked coins; achondroplasia FGFR3) → hypertrophy (chondrocytes swell, Ca²⁺ matrix; vitamin D deficiency/rickets here) → calcification → ossification.</span></li>\n<li>Fracture repair: hematoma → soft callus (fibrocartilage) → hard callus (woven bone) → remodeling (lamellar).</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Disorder</th><th>Defect</th><th>Labs / clue</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Osteoporosis</td><td>↓ bone mass, normal mineralization</td><td>Normal Ca, PO₄, ALP, PTH; vertebral crush, hip, Colles fx</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Osteopetrosis</td><td>Osteoclast failure (carbonic anhydrase II)</td><td>Dense brittle bone, marrow failure, Erlenmeyer flask</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Osteomalacia / rickets</td><td>Defective mineralization (vit D)</td><td>↓ Ca, ↓ PO₄, ↑ ALP, ↑ PTH; rachitic rosary, bowed legs</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Paget disease</td><td>Excess osteoclast then blast activity</td><td>Isolated ↑↑ ALP, mosaic woven bone, ↑ hat size, hearing loss</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Osteogenesis imperfecta</td><td>Type I collagen</td><td>Blue sclerae, multiple fractures, hearing loss</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Achondroplasia</td><td>FGFR3 gain (AD) — ↓ proliferation zone</td><td>Short limbs, normal trunk; ↑ paternal age</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Connective tissue &amp; ECM</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Cells: fibroblasts (main), macrophages, mast cells (heparin, histamine; IgE), plasma cells, adipocytes. Fibers: collagen, reticular (type III), elastic. Ground substance: GAGs, proteoglycans, glycoproteins (fibronectin, laminin).</li>\n<li>Loose (areolar, under epithelia) vs dense irregular (dermis, capsules) vs <span class=\"hy\">dense regular (tendons, ligaments — parallel type I, few fibroblasts, poor blood supply)</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Collagen</th><th>Location</th><th>Disease</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>I (most abundant)</td><td><b>B</b>one, sk<b>in</b>, tendon, dentin, fascia, cornea, late wound repair</td><td>Osteogenesis imperfecta, Ehlers–Danlos (some)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>II</td><td>Cartilage, vitreous, nucleus pulposus</td><td>Achondrogenesis/Stickler</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>III (reticulin)</td><td>Skin, vessels, uterus, granulation tissue, lymphoid organs</td><td>Vascular Ehlers–Danlos</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>IV</td><td>Basement membrane</td><td>Alport (hearing, eye, kidney), Goodpasture (anti-α3 IV)</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"mn\">“Be (So Totally) Cool, Read Books”: type I Bone; II Cartilage; III Reticulin; IV Basement membrane.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elastin: rich in glycine, proline, <b>desmosine</b> cross-links (lysyl oxidase), scaffold = <b>fibrillin-1</b>. <span class=\"hy\">Marfan = fibrillin-1 (FBN1)</span>: tall, arachnodactyly, lens subluxation upward, aortic root dilation/dissection, MVP. α₁-antitrypsin deficiency → elastase destroys lung elastin (panacinar emphysema).</li>\n<li>Collagen synthesis &amp; disease details → MSK Biochemistry guide.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Skin</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Epidermis layers (deep → superficial): stratum basale (stem cells, melanocytes) → spinosum (desmosomes “spines”, Langerhans cells) → granulosum (keratohyalin, lamellar bodies) → lucidum (thick skin only) → corneum (dead keratinized)</span> <span class=\"mn\">(“Californians Like Girls in String Bikinis”: corneum, lucidum, granulosum, spinosum, basale)</span>.</li>\n<li>Cells: keratinocytes (~90%), <span class=\"hy\">melanocytes (neural crest; melanin to keratinocytes via melanosomes; albinism = normal number, ↓ tyrosinase; vitiligo = ↓ number)</span>, <span class=\"hy\">Langerhans cells (antigen-presenting; Birbeck “tennis racket” granules; CD1a, S-100; bone marrow origin)</span>, <b>Merkel cells</b> (touch, basale).</li>\n<li>Dermis: papillary (loose CT, Meissner corpuscles) and reticular (dense irregular, Pacinian corpuscles deeper, glands). Hypodermis = adipose.</li>\n<li>Thick skin (palms/soles): stratum lucidum, no hair. Thin skin: hair follicles, sebaceous glands.</li>\n<li>Glands: <span class=\"hy\">eccrine sweat (merocrine; everywhere; sympathetic <b>cholinergic</b>)</span>, apocrine (axilla, groin; open into follicles; after puberty), sebaceous (holocrine; acne).</li>\n<li>Receptors: Meissner (light touch, papillary dermis, glabrous), Pacinian (vibration/pressure, deep, onion layers), Merkel (sustained pressure), Ruffini (stretch), free nerve endings (pain, temperature).</li>\n<li>Psoriasis: thickened spinosum (acanthosis), parakeratosis, Munro microabscesses. Burns: 1st degree epidermis; 2nd into dermis (blisters); 3rd full thickness (painless).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Slide identification tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">Peripheral multiple nuclei + striations = skeletal; central nucleus + branching + intercalated discs = cardiac; spindle cells with central nucleus, no striations = smooth.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Concentric lamellae around a central canal = osteon (compact bone). Lacunae with isogenous groups = cartilage (glassy = hyaline; dark fibers = elastic; rows of cells between collagen = fibrocartilage).</li>\n<li>Large multinucleated cell in a Howship lacuna = osteoclast; cuboidal row on osteoid = osteoblasts.</li>\n<li>Parallel collagen with flattened nuclei in rows = tendon (dense regular).</li>\n<li>Keratinized stratified squamous with thick corneum and lucidum = thick skin.</li>\n</ul>", "msk-physiology": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Neuromuscular junction (NMJ)</h2>\n<ol style=\"padding-left:16px;margin:2px 0 4px\">\n<li>AP reaches motor nerve terminal → <span class=\"hy\">voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels (P/Q type) open → Ca²⁺ influx</span>.</li>\n<li>Ca²⁺ triggers <b>SNARE</b>-mediated exocytosis of ACh vesicles (synaptobrevin, syntaxin, SNAP-25).</li>\n<li>ACh binds <span class=\"hy\">nicotinic receptors (N<sub>M</sub>, ligand-gated cation channels; Na⁺ in &gt; K⁺ out)</span> on the motor end plate (junctional folds).</li>\n<li>End-plate potential (graded) → always reaches threshold (high safety factor) → muscle AP.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Acetylcholinesterase</span> in the synaptic cleft hydrolyzes ACh → acetate + choline (choline recycled).</li>\n</ol>\n<table><tr><th>Agent / disease</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Botulinum toxin</td><td>Cleaves SNAREs → blocks ACh release</td><td>Descending flaccid paralysis; infant botulism (honey); cosmetic/dystonia use</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Myasthenia gravis</td><td>Antibodies vs postsynaptic nicotinic ACh receptors</td><td>Fatigable weakness, ptosis, diplopia, <b>worse with use</b>; thymoma; edrophonium/AChE inhibitor test; treat pyridostigmine</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Lambert–Eaton</td><td>Antibodies vs presynaptic voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels</td><td>Proximal weakness <b>improves with repeated use</b>, autonomic sx, ↓ reflexes; small cell lung cancer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Curare / d-tubocurarine, rocuronium</td><td>Competitive nicotinic antagonists (nondepolarizing)</td><td>Paralysis; reversed by AChE inhibitors (neostigmine) / sugammadex</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Succinylcholine</td><td>Depolarizing agonist; persistent depolarization inactivates Na⁺ channels</td><td>Fasciculations then flaccid paralysis; malignant hyperthermia, hyperkalemia</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Organophosphates / neostigmine</td><td>Inhibit AChE</td><td>↑ ACh; organophosphate poisoning = DUMBBELSS; atropine + pralidoxime</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hemicholinium</td><td>Blocks choline reuptake</td><td>↓ ACh synthesis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>α-bungarotoxin</td><td>Irreversibly binds nicotinic receptor</td><td>Paralysis</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Skeletal muscle structure &amp; excitation–contraction coupling</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Sarcomere (Z to Z): <span class=\"hy\">A band constant; I band and H zone shorten</span> (sliding filament). Titin = elasticity/centering; nebulin = actin length ruler.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">E–C coupling (skeletal): muscle AP spreads down T-tubules → DHPR (L-type Ca²⁺ channel acting as a voltage sensor) is <b>mechanically coupled</b> to RyR1 on the SR → Ca²⁺ release → binds troponin C → tropomyosin moves off myosin-binding sites → cross-bridge cycling.</span> Extracellular Ca²⁺ is NOT needed in skeletal muscle.</li>\n<li><b>Cardiac</b>: Ca²⁺-induced Ca²⁺ release — Ca²⁺ entering through DHPR triggers RyR2 (extracellular Ca²⁺ required).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Relaxation: SERCA pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR (uses ATP)</span>; phospholamban regulates SERCA in heart. Calsequestrin stores Ca²⁺ in SR.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Malignant hyperthermia</span>: RyR1 mutation (AD); triggered by volatile anesthetics/succinylcholine → uncontrolled Ca²⁺ release → rigidity, hyperthermia, hypercarbia, hyperkalemia, ↑ CK → <b>dantrolene</b> (blocks RyR1).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box\"><b class=\"t\">Cross-bridge cycle (ATP roles):</b> (1) Rigor: myosin bound to actin, no ATP. (2) <b>ATP binds myosin → myosin releases actin.</b> (3) ATP hydrolysis (ADP + Pi stay bound) → myosin head “cocked”. (4) Head binds actin (once Ca²⁺ exposes sites). (5) <b>Pi release → power stroke</b>; ADP released. <span class=\"hy\">No ATP → rigor mortis (heads stuck to actin).</span> ATP is also used by SERCA (relaxation) and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Contraction mechanics</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Twitch</b>: single AP → single contraction (latent, contraction, relaxation). AP lasts ~2–5 ms, twitch much longer → summation possible.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Summation &amp; tetanus</span>: ↑ stimulation frequency → Ca²⁺ stays elevated → summation → <b>fused tetanus</b> (max force). <b>Treppe</b> (staircase): slightly ↑ force with repeated twitches.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Recruitment (size principle)</span>: small motor units (type I, slow, fatigue-resistant) recruited first, large (type II) last. Force graded by recruitment + rate coding.</li>\n<li>Motor unit = one α motor neuron + all fibers it innervates; small units for fine control (eye muscles), large for gross (quadriceps).</li>\n<li><b>Isometric</b> (length constant, tension ↑) vs <b>isotonic</b> (constant load, length changes): concentric (shortening), <b>eccentric</b> (lengthening under load — most force, most DOMS/damage).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Length–tension relationship</span>: max active tension at optimal sarcomere length (~2.0–2.2 µm, maximal cross-bridge overlap). Too short (filaments overlap/collide) or too long (little overlap) → ↓ active tension. Passive tension (titin, CT) ↑ with stretch; total = active + passive.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Force–velocity relationship</span>: ↑ load → ↓ shortening velocity; Vmax at zero load; at max (isometric) load velocity = 0. Power max at ~1/3 Vmax.</li>\n<li>Preload = load before contraction (sets length); afterload = load the muscle works against.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Energetics &amp; fiber types</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>ATP stores last only seconds. Sources: <span class=\"hy\">phosphocreatine (creatine kinase) — fastest, ~10 s</span> → anaerobic glycolysis (→ lactate; ~1–2 min) → oxidative phosphorylation (sustained, needs O₂).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Fenn effect</span>: muscle doing more work consumes more ATP/energy (energy liberated ∝ work performed).</li>\n<li>Efficiency ~20–25% (rest lost as heat).</li>\n<li>Fatigue: ↓ glycogen, ↑ Pi, ↑ H⁺, impaired Ca²⁺ release; central fatigue.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th></th><th class=\"hy\">Type I (slow oxidative)</th><th>Type IIa (fast oxidative-glycolytic)</th><th class=\"hy\">Type IIx/IIb (fast glycolytic)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Color</td><td>Red (myoglobin)</td><td>Pink/red</td><td>White</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mitochondria / capillaries</td><td>Many</td><td>Many</td><td>Few</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Myosin ATPase speed</td><td>Slow</td><td>Fast</td><td>Fastest</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fatigue</td><td>Resistant</td><td>Intermediate</td><td>Fast</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fiber size / motor unit</td><td>Small</td><td>Medium</td><td>Large</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Use</td><td>Posture (soleus), endurance</td><td>—</td><td>Sprint, power, extraocular-like fast movement</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"mn\">“One slow red ox”: type 1, slow, red, oxidative. Endurance training ↑ mitochondria/type I traits; resistance training → hypertrophy of type II.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Exercise physiology</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>↑ Cardiac output (HR and SV), ↓ TPR (local metabolic vasodilation: adenosine, K⁺, H⁺, CO₂, lactate, NO), ↑ systolic BP, diastolic BP ~same or ↓.</li>\n<li>Ventilation ↑ proportionally (PaO₂, PaCO₂ normal in moderate exercise); ↑ O₂ extraction (rightward shift of O₂–Hb curve: ↑ temperature, CO₂, H⁺, 2,3-BPG).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Oxygen debt (EPOC)</span>: extra O₂ after exercise to restore phosphocreatine, ATP, lactate clearance (Cori cycle), myoglobin O₂.</li>\n<li>Lactate threshold: point where lactate accumulates (anaerobic glycolysis predominates).</li>\n<li>Training: ↑ VO₂max, ↑ stroke volume, ↓ resting HR, ↑ capillary density, ↑ mitochondria.</li>\n<li>Rhabdomyolysis (crush, extreme exertion, statins): ↑ CK, myoglobinuria (urine + blood on dipstick, no RBCs) → AKI; hyperkalemia.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Smooth muscle</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>No sarcomeres/troponin; actin anchored to <b>dense bodies</b> (α-actinin) and membrane; intermediate filaments (desmin, vimentin). Little SR; caveolae.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Contraction: ↑ intracellular Ca²⁺ (from ECF via L-type channels, and SR via IP₃ receptors) → Ca²⁺–calmodulin → activates myosin light-chain kinase (MLCK) → phosphorylates myosin light chain → cross-bridge cycling.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Relaxation: myosin light-chain phosphatase (MLCP) dephosphorylates myosin.</span> NO → ↑ cGMP → PKG → activates MLCP → relaxation (nitrates, sildenafil). β₂ / cAMP → PKA → inhibits MLCK → relaxation (albuterol).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Latch state</span>: dephosphorylated myosin remains attached → sustained tension with little ATP (sphincters, vascular tone).</li>\n<li>Ca²⁺ sources: voltage-gated (L-type; blocked by verapamil, nifedipine) = electromechanical; <b>pharmacomechanical</b> = receptor (α₁, M₃) → Gq → PLC → IP₃ → SR Ca²⁺ release (no AP needed). Rho kinase inhibits MLCP → Ca²⁺ sensitization.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Single-unit (visceral)</span>: gap junctions, contract as a syncytium, pacemaker/slow waves, stretch-activated — GI tract, uterus, bladder, small vessels. <b>Multi-unit</b>: each fiber innervated separately, fine control — iris, ciliary muscle, vas deferens, piloerector, large arteries.</li>\n<li>Slow waves (GI, interstitial cells of Cajal) set rhythm; spike potentials (Ca²⁺) cause contraction.</li>\n<li>Slower, more economical, can contract over a wide length range (stress–relaxation/plasticity — bladder fills without pressure rise).</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Skeletal</th><th>Smooth</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Ca²⁺ sensor</td><td class=\"hy\">Troponin C</td><td class=\"hy\">Calmodulin → MLCK</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ca²⁺ source</td><td>SR only (mechanical DHPR–RyR)</td><td>ECF + SR (IP₃)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Regulation</td><td>Thin-filament</td><td>Thick-filament (myosin phosphorylation)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed / ATP use</td><td>Fast / high</td><td>Slow / low (latch)</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Rapid-fire NBME pearls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">Myasthenia = postsynaptic AChR Ab (worse with use); Lambert–Eaton = presynaptic Ca²⁺ channel Ab (better with use, SCLC).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Botulinum blocks ACh release (SNAREs); organophosphates block AChE; succinylcholine depolarizing block.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Skeletal E–C: DHPR mechanically opens RyR1; cardiac: Ca²⁺-induced Ca²⁺ release.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">ATP binding releases myosin from actin; no ATP = rigor. SERCA relaxes muscle.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Malignant hyperthermia: RyR1, volatile anesthetics/succinylcholine → dantrolene.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Smooth muscle: Ca²⁺–calmodulin–MLCK; relax via MLCP (NO/cGMP); latch state.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Size principle: small type I units first. A band constant.</li>\n</ul>", "msk-biochem": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Glucose uptake</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Transporter</th><th>Location</th><th>Note</th></tr>\n<tr><td>GLUT1</td><td>RBCs, brain, BBB, placenta</td><td>Insulin-independent, basal uptake</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GLUT2</td><td>Liver, pancreatic β cells, kidney &amp; intestine (basolateral)</td><td>High Km — glucose sensor, bidirectional</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GLUT3</td><td>Neurons, placenta</td><td>High affinity</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>GLUT4</td><td>Skeletal muscle, adipose, heart</td><td>Insulin-dependent (vesicles move to membrane); exercise also translocates GLUT4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GLUT5</td><td>Intestine, sperm</td><td>Fructose</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Glycolysis (cytosol; all cells)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Glucose → 2 pyruvate; net <b>2 ATP + 2 NADH</b> (anaerobic: pyruvate → lactate via LDH regenerates NAD⁺; RBCs have no mitochondria — glycolysis only).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Irreversible, regulated steps: hexokinase/glucokinase, PFK-1 (rate-limiting), pyruvate kinase.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Enzyme</th><th>Activators</th><th>Inhibitors</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Hexokinase (most tissues; low Km, high affinity)</td><td>—</td><td class=\"hy\">Glucose-6-phosphate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Glucokinase (liver, β cells; high Km, high Vmax)</td><td>Insulin</td><td>F-6-P (via GK regulatory protein)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>PFK-1 (rate-limiting)</td><td>AMP, fructose-2,6-bisphosphate</td><td>ATP, citrate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pyruvate kinase</td><td class=\"hy\">Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate (feedforward)</td><td>ATP, alanine, glucagon (PKA phosphorylation in liver)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pyruvate dehydrogenase (mitochondria)</td><td>NAD⁺, ADP, Ca²⁺ (exercising muscle), insulin</td><td>Acetyl-CoA, NADH, ATP</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Fructose-2,6-BP (made by PFK-2)</span>: fed state/insulin ↑ PFK-2 activity → ↑ F-2,6-BP → ↑ glycolysis; fasting/glucagon → ↑ FBPase-2 → ↓ F-2,6-BP → gluconeogenesis.</li>\n<li>Substrate-level phosphorylation steps: <b>phosphoglycerate kinase</b> (1,3-BPG → 3-PG) and <b>pyruvate kinase</b> (PEP → pyruvate). NADH made at G3P dehydrogenase.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">PDH complex cofactors: TPP (B1), lipoic acid, CoA (B5), FAD (B2), NAD⁺ (B3)</span> <span class=\"mn\">(“Tender Loving Care For Nancy”)</span>. Arsenic inhibits lipoic acid. Same cofactors for α-KG DH.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Pyruvate kinase deficiency</span>: most common glycolytic enzyme defect → hemolytic anemia (RBCs can’t make ATP), echinocytes; ↑ 2,3-BPG.</li>\n<li>2,3-BPG made in RBCs via the Rapoport–Luebering shunt → right shift of O₂ curve.</li>\n<li>Pyruvate fates: lactate (LDH; anaerobic), alanine (ALT), oxaloacetate (pyruvate carboxylase, biotin; gluconeogenesis), acetyl-CoA (PDH; TCA).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cori cycle</span>: muscle/RBC lactate → liver → glucose (costs 4 net ATP in liver). Glucose–alanine cycle carries N to liver.</li>\n<li><b>Arsenic</b> also inhibits glyceraldehyde-3-P dehydrogenase step (arsenate substitutes for Pi → no ATP from 1,3-BPG). <b>Fluoride</b> inhibits enolase (used in blood glucose tubes).</li>\n<li>Fructose: fructokinase → F-1-P → aldolase B (bypasses PFK-1 → fast metabolism). <span class=\"hy\">Hereditary fructose intolerance (aldolase B)</span>: hypoglycemia, vomiting, liver failure after fruit/sucrose. Essential fructosuria (fructokinase) benign. Galactose: <span class=\"hy\">classic galactosemia (GALT)</span> → cataracts, liver failure, E. coli sepsis; galactokinase deficiency → cataracts only.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Glycogen metabolism</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Stored in liver (~10% weight; maintains blood glucose) and muscle (~1–2%; fuels muscle only). <span class=\"hy\">Muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase</span> → can’t release free glucose.</li>\n<li><b>Synthesis</b>: glucose-6-P → glucose-1-P (phosphoglucomutase) → <span class=\"hy\">UDP-glucose</span> (UDP-glucose pyrophosphorylase) → glycogenin primer → <span class=\"hy\">glycogen synthase (rate-limiting; α1→4)</span> → branching enzyme (α1→6, every ~8–10 residues).</li>\n<li><b>Breakdown</b>: <span class=\"hy\">glycogen phosphorylase (rate-limiting; needs PLP/B6)</span> releases glucose-1-P from α1→4 until 4 residues from a branch → <b>debranching enzyme</b> (4-α-glucanotransferase moves 3 residues; α-1,6-glucosidase releases the branch glucose as free glucose) → glucose-1-P → glucose-6-P → (liver) glucose-6-phosphatase → glucose.</li>\n<li>Lysosomal α-1,4-glucosidase (acid maltase) degrades a small fraction of glycogen in lysosomes.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Insulin (fed)</td><td>Activates protein phosphatase 1 → <b>dephosphorylates</b>: synthase ON, phosphorylase OFF → glycogen synthesis</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Glucagon (liver), epinephrine (liver + muscle)</td><td>cAMP → PKA → phosphorylase kinase → <b>phosphorylates</b>: phosphorylase ON, synthase OFF → glycogenolysis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Muscle contraction</td><td>Ca²⁺–calmodulin activates phosphorylase kinase; AMP allosterically activates muscle phosphorylase</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Liver glucose</td><td>Glucose inhibits liver phosphorylase; G-6-P activates synthase</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"mn\">Phosphorylation = breakdown mode (glycogenolysis); dephosphorylation = storage mode.</p>\n\n<h3>Glycogen storage diseases — MUST KNOW</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Enzyme</th><th>Key findings</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>I — Von Gierke</td><td>Glucose-6-phosphatase (Ia) / transporter (Ib)</td><td>Severe <b>fasting hypoglycemia</b>, ↑↑ glycogen in liver &amp; kidney (hepatomegaly, renomegaly), <b>lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia (gout), hyperlipidemia</b>, doll-like face; can’t respond to glucagon. Tx: frequent cornstarch feeds; avoid fructose/galactose.</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>II — Pompe</td><td>Lysosomal acid α-1,4-glucosidase (acid maltase)</td><td><b>Cardiomegaly</b>, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, hypotonia, macroglossia, early death (infantile); normal blood glucose. <span class=\"mn\">“Pompe trashes the Pump (heart, liver, muscle)”</span>. Enzyme replacement.</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>III — Cori</td><td>Debranching enzyme (α-1,6-glucosidase)</td><td>Milder Von Gierke: hypoglycemia, hepatomegaly, ± myopathy, <b>normal lactate</b>; short outer chains (limit dextrin) accumulate; gluconeogenesis intact.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>IV — Andersen</td><td>Branching enzyme</td><td>Abnormal long unbranched glycogen → cirrhosis, liver failure, death in early childhood.</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>V — McArdle</td><td>Muscle glycogen phosphorylase (myophosphorylase)</td><td><b>Exercise intolerance, painful cramps, myoglobinuria</b> (red urine) after exercise, ↑ CK, <b>flat venous lactate curve</b> on ischemic forearm test, “second wind” phenomenon; normal blood glucose.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>VI — Hers</td><td>Liver glycogen phosphorylase</td><td>Mild hypoglycemia, hepatomegaly.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>VII — Tarui</td><td>Muscle PFK-1</td><td>Like McArdle + hemolytic anemia.</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"mn\">“Very Poor Carbohydrate Metabolism” → Von Gierke (I), Pompe (II), Cori (III), McArdle (V). All AR.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. GAGs, proteoglycans &amp; carbohydrates in MSK</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>GAGs = repeating disaccharides (amino sugar + uronic acid), <b>negatively charged</b> → attract Na⁺ and water → <span class=\"hy\">resist compression</span> (cartilage); proteoglycan = core protein + GAGs.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Aggrecan</span> (cartilage): chondroitin sulfate + keratan sulfate on a core protein, attached by link protein to <b>hyaluronan</b> → huge aggregates.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Hyaluronic acid = only non-sulfated GAG, not bound to protein; synovial fluid viscosity, vitreous.</span> Heparin = only intracellular GAG (mast cells). Chondroitin sulfate most abundant (cartilage, bone). Keratan sulfate (cornea, cartilage). Dermatan sulfate (skin, vessels, valves). Heparan sulfate (basement membranes).</li>\n<li>Synthesis: core protein in RER → GAG chains added and sulfated in Golgi (PAPS sulfate donor). Degradation in lysosomes.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Mucopolysaccharidoses</span> (lysosomal GAG degradation defects; urinary GAGs, coarse facies, skeletal dysplasia): <b>Hurler</b> (α-L-iduronidase, AR) — corneal clouding, gargoylism, intellectual disability, early death; <b>Hunter</b> (iduronate sulfatase, X-linked) — no corneal clouding, aggressive behavior <span class=\"mn\">(“Hunters see clearly and aim for the X”)</span>; heparan + dermatan sulfate accumulate.</li>\n<li>Osteoarthritis: ↓ proteoglycans → cartilage loses water-holding capacity; glucosamine/chondroitin supplements.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Collagen</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Structure: <span class=\"hy\">triple helix of α chains with Gly-X-Y repeats (glycine every 3rd residue — only AA small enough for the center); X/Y often proline and hydroxyproline/hydroxylysine.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Location</th><th>Detail / disease</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. Translation of preprocollagen</td><td>RER</td><td>Signal peptide cleaved</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>2. Hydroxylation of Pro &amp; Lys</td><td>RER</td><td>Prolyl &amp; lysyl hydroxylase need <b>vitamin C</b> (+ Fe²⁺, O₂, α-KG) → <b>scurvy</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. Glycosylation of hydroxylysine</td><td>RER</td><td>Galactose/glucose added</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>4. Triple helix (procollagen) via disulfides at C-propeptides</td><td>RER</td><td><b>Osteogenesis imperfecta</b> — can’t form triple helix (Gly substitutions)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5. Secretion</td><td>Golgi → exocytosis</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>6. Propeptide cleavage → tropocollagen</td><td>Extracellular</td><td>Procollagen peptidases; defect → <b>Ehlers–Danlos (dermatosparaxis)</b></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>7. Cross-linking → fibrils</td><td>Extracellular</td><td><b>Lysyl oxidase (copper)</b> covalent cross-links; <b>Menkes disease</b> (ATP7A, ↓ Cu: kinky brittle hair, hypotonia, growth delay); Ehlers–Danlos; lathyrism</td></tr></table>\n<table><tr><th>Disease</th><th>Defect</th><th>Features</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Scurvy</td><td>Vitamin C → ↓ hydroxylation</td><td>Swollen bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage, corkscrew hairs, poor wound healing, hemarthrosis</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Osteogenesis imperfecta</td><td>Type I collagen (COL1A1/2), AD</td><td>Multiple fractures with minimal trauma (mimics child abuse), <b>blue sclerae</b>, hearing loss (otosclerosis), dentinogenesis imperfecta</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Ehlers–Danlos</td><td>Collagen processing (classic: type V; vascular: type III)</td><td>Hyperextensible skin, hypermobile joints, easy bruising; vascular type → arterial/organ rupture, berry aneurysms</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Menkes</td><td>Copper transport (ATP7A), XR</td><td>↓ lysyl oxidase → brittle kinky hair, neurodegeneration</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Alport</td><td>Type IV collagen</td><td>Hematuria/nephritis, sensorineural deafness, lens/eye defects <span class=\"mn\">(“can’t see, can’t pee, can’t hear”)</span></td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"mn\">Collagen types: I Bone/skin/tendon; II Cartilage; III Reticulin (vessels, granulation); IV Basement membrane.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Elastin</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Stretchy protein in lungs, large arteries, elastic ligaments (ligamentum flavum), skin, vocal cords.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Rich in nonhydroxylated proline, glycine and lysine; lysyl oxidase forms desmosine cross-links</span>; assembled on a <b>fibrillin</b> scaffold. Broken down by elastase (inhibited by α₁-antitrypsin).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Marfan syndrome</span>: FBN1 (fibrillin-1), AD — tall, long limbs, arachnodactyly, pectus, <b>upward lens subluxation</b>, MVP, <b>aortic root dilation → dissection</b>, hypermobility. (Homocystinuria: downward lens dislocation, thrombosis, intellectual disability.)</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">α₁-antitrypsin deficiency</span>: unopposed elastase → panacinar emphysema (lower lobes, young, nonsmoker) + cirrhosis (misfolded PiZ retained in hepatocyte ER, PAS+ globules).</li>\n<li>Wrinkles of aging: ↓ collagen &amp; elastin synthesis, UV damage (solar elastosis).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Rapid-fire NBME pearls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">PFK-1 rate-limiting (activated by AMP, F-2,6-BP; inhibited by ATP, citrate); pyruvate kinase deficiency → hemolysis.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Glycogen synthase / phosphorylase are rate-limiting; insulin dephosphorylates (store), glucagon/epi phosphorylate (break down).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Von Gierke: hypoglycemia + lactic acidosis + gout + hepatomegaly. Pompe: cardiomegaly (lysosomal). Cori: Von Gierke-like but normal lactate. McArdle: cramps, myoglobinuria, flat lactate.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Vitamin C → hydroxylation (scurvy); copper → lysyl oxidase cross-links (Menkes); OI = type I collagen, blue sclerae.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Marfan = fibrillin-1 (upward lens, aortic root); hyaluronic acid non-sulfated; Hunter = X-linked, no corneal clouding.</li>\n</ul>", "msk-anatomy": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Back, shoulder &amp; scapular region</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Muscle</th><th>Nerve</th><th>Action / lesion</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Trapezius</td><td>Spinal accessory (CN XI)</td><td>Elevates/retracts/rotates scapula up; CN XI injury (posterior triangle surgery/node biopsy) → shoulder droop, can’t shrug or abduct above 90°</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Latissimus dorsi</td><td>Thoracodorsal (C6–8)</td><td>Extends, adducts, medially rotates arm</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Levator scapulae, rhomboids</td><td>Dorsal scapular (C5)</td><td>Elevate/retract scapula; lesion → mild lateral scapular winging</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Serratus anterior</td><td>Long thoracic (C5–7)</td><td>Protracts, upward rotates scapula (abduction &gt;90°); lesion (mastectomy/axillary dissection, stab) → <b>medial winged scapula</b> pushing against wall</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Deltoid</td><td>Axillary (C5–6)</td><td>Abducts 15–90°; lesion → flat shoulder, ↓ abduction, numb “regimental badge” area</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Supraspinatus</td><td>Suprascapular</td><td>Initiates abduction (0–15°); <b>most commonly torn rotator cuff</b>; impingement under acromion; empty-can test, painful arc</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Infraspinatus</td><td>Suprascapular</td><td>Lateral rotation (pitching injury)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Teres minor</td><td>Axillary</td><td>Lateral rotation, adduction</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Subscapularis</td><td>Upper &amp; lower subscapular</td><td>Medial rotation, adduction (lift-off test)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Teres major</td><td>Lower subscapular</td><td>Medial rotation, adduction, extension</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Rotator cuff = SITS</span> (Supraspinatus, Infraspinatus, Teres minor, Subscapularis); all insert on greater tubercle except subscapularis (lesser).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Quadrangular space</span> (teres minor, teres major, long head triceps, humerus): <b>axillary nerve + posterior circumflex humeral artery</b>. Triangular space: circumflex scapular artery. <b>Triangular interval</b>: radial nerve + profunda brachii.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Shoulder dislocation is usually anterior (inferior)</span> → axillary nerve at risk. Posterior dislocation: seizures/electrocution.</li>\n<li>Clavicle fracture (middle third): medial fragment up (SCM), lateral fragment down (arm weight) and in (pec major). Triangle of auscultation: trapezius, latissimus, scapula.</li>\n<li>Scapular anastomosis (subclavian ↔ axillary) bypasses axillary/subclavian obstruction.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Brachial plexus &amp; spinal levels</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Roots C5–T1 → Trunks (upper C5–6, middle C7, lower C8–T1) → Divisions (anterior/posterior) → Cords (lateral, posterior, medial, named relative to axillary artery) → Branches</span> <span class=\"mn\">(“Randy Travis Drinks Cold Beer”)</span>.</li>\n<li>Roots give <b>dorsal scapular</b> (C5) and <b>long thoracic</b> (C5–7); upper trunk gives <b>suprascapular</b> and nerve to subclavius.</li>\n<li>Lateral cord → musculocutaneous, lateral pectoral, lateral root of median. Medial cord → ulnar, medial pectoral, medial cutaneous nerves, medial root of median. <span class=\"hy\">Posterior cord → axillary, radial, upper/lower subscapular, thoracodorsal</span> <span class=\"mn\">(STAR: Subscapulars, Thoracodorsal, Axillary, Radial)</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Injury</th><th>Cause</th><th>Roots</th><th>Picture</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Erb palsy (upper trunk)</td><td>Infant lateral neck traction in delivery; adult fall on shoulder</td><td>C5–C6</td><td><b>“Waiter’s tip”</b>: arm adducted (deltoid, supraspinatus), medially rotated (infraspinatus), elbow extended (biceps/brachialis), forearm pronated</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Klumpke palsy (lower trunk)</td><td>Upward arm traction (grabbing branch, delivery with arm up); Pancoast tumor, cervical rib</td><td>C8–T1</td><td><b>Total claw hand</b> (intrinsic loss), ± <b>Horner syndrome</b> (T1 sympathetics)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Thoracic outlet syndrome</td><td>Cervical rib, scalene compression</td><td>Lower trunk + subclavian vessels</td><td>Hand intrinsic atrophy, ischemia, pain</td></tr></table>\n<table><tr><th>Reflex</th><th>Root</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Biceps</td><td>C5 (C5–6)</td></tr><tr class=\"hy\"><td>Brachioradialis</td><td>C6</td></tr><tr class=\"hy\"><td>Triceps</td><td>C7</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Patellar</td><td>L4 (L3–4)</td></tr><tr class=\"hy\"><td>Achilles</td><td>S1</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"mn\">Reflexes count up: S1–2 ankle, L3–4 knee, C5–6 biceps, C7–8 triceps. Dermatomes: C6 thumb, C7 middle finger, C8 little finger, T4 nipple, T10 umbilicus, L4 knee/medial leg, L5 dorsum of foot, S1 lateral foot/sole.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Arm, forearm &amp; elbow</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Anterior arm</b> (musculocutaneous): biceps (supination &gt; flexion; radial tuberosity), brachialis (main elbow flexor), coracobrachialis. <b>Posterior arm</b> (radial): triceps, anconeus.</li>\n<li><b>Anterior forearm</b> (median, except FCU and medial half FDP = ulnar): pronators, wrist &amp; finger flexors from <b>medial epicondyle</b> (golfer’s elbow). <b>Posterior forearm</b> (radial/PIN): extensors from <b>lateral epicondyle</b> (<span class=\"hy\">tennis elbow = ECRB</span>). Brachioradialis = radial nerve but flexes elbow (midprone).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Supination: biceps (most powerful) + supinator. Pronation: pronator teres + pronator quadratus.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cubital fossa (lateral → medial): biceps tendon, brachial artery, median nerve</span> <span class=\"mn\">(“TAN”: Tendon, Artery, Nerve; radial nerve lateral)</span>. Median cubital vein superficial (venipuncture). Boundaries: brachioradialis (lateral), pronator teres (medial).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Supracondylar humerus fracture</span> (children, FOOSH): brachial artery + median nerve injury → Volkmann ischemic contracture (compartment syndrome).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Nursemaid’s elbow</span>: radial head subluxation out of the annular ligament (pulling a child’s arm).</li>\n<li>Elbow joint: humeroulnar (hinge), humeroradial; proximal radioulnar (pivot). UCL (Tommy John) resists valgus.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Hand &amp; wrist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Carpal bones: <span class=\"mn\">“So Long To Pinky, Here Comes The Thumb”</span> — proximal row lateral → medial: Scaphoid, Lunate, Triquetrum, Pisiform; distal row medial → lateral: Hamate, Capitate, Trapezoid, Trapezium.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Scaphoid = most commonly fractured carpal</span> (FOOSH; tender in anatomical snuffbox; may be X-ray occult) → <b>avascular necrosis</b> of proximal pole (retrograde blood supply) and nonunion. <span class=\"hy\">Lunate = most commonly dislocated</span> (anteriorly) → acute carpal tunnel syndrome. Hook of hamate fracture → ulnar nerve (Guyon canal).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Carpal tunnel</span> (flexor retinaculum roof): median nerve + 9 tendons (4 FDS, 4 FDP, FPL). FCR not inside (separate). Compression (pregnancy, RA, hypothyroid, diabetes, amyloid, repetitive use) → numb lateral 3½ digits, <b>thenar atrophy</b>, Tinel/Phalen positive; <b>palm sensation spared</b> (palmar cutaneous branch passes superficial to retinaculum).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Guyon canal</span> (pisiform &amp; hook of hamate): ulnar nerve + artery (cyclists).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Anatomical snuffbox</span>: lateral = APL &amp; EPB; medial = EPL; floor = scaphoid &amp; trapezium; contains <b>radial artery</b> (and cephalic vein origin, superficial radial nerve over roof).</li>\n<li><b>Thenar</b> (median recurrent branch): APB, FPB (superficial head), opponens pollicis. <b>Hypothenar</b> (ulnar): ADM, FDMB, ODM. <b>Adductor pollicis</b>, all interossei, lumbricals 3–4 = <b>ulnar</b>; lumbricals 1–2 = median.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Interossei: PAD (palmar adduct), DAB (dorsal abduct)</span>; lumbricals flex MCP + extend IP. Card test (palmar interossei, ulnar).</li>\n<li>Extensor compartments (6): APL/EPB (de Quervain tenosynovitis, Finkelstein test) | ECRL/ECRB | EPL (around Lister tubercle) | ED/EI | EDM | ECU.</li>\n<li>Deformities: mallet finger (terminal extensor), boutonnière (central slip), swan-neck (RA), Dupuytren (palmar fascia), jersey finger (FDP avulsion, ring finger), gamekeeper/skier thumb (UCL of thumb MCP).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Upper limb nerve injuries — MUST KNOW</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Nerve</th><th>Injured by</th><th>Motor loss / sign</th><th>Sensory loss</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Axillary (C5–6)</td><td>Surgical neck fracture, anterior shoulder dislocation</td><td>Deltoid (abduction 15–90°), teres minor; flat shoulder</td><td>Lateral shoulder (“regimental badge”)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Musculocutaneous (C5–7)</td><td>Upper trunk compression</td><td>Biceps, brachialis, coracobrachialis → weak elbow flexion/supination</td><td>Lateral forearm</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Radial (C5–T1)</td><td>Midshaft humerus fracture (radial groove), “Saturday night palsy”, crutches (axilla), radial head fracture (PIN)</td><td><b>Wrist drop</b>, ↓ finger/thumb extension; axillary lesion also triceps loss; midshaft spares triceps. <b>PIN</b>: finger drop, wrist extends with radial deviation (ECRL spared), no sensory loss</td><td>Posterior arm/forearm, dorsal lateral hand &amp; <b>first dorsal web space</b></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Median (C5–T1)</td><td>Supracondylar fracture (proximal), carpal tunnel/wrist laceration (distal)</td><td>Proximal: <b>“hand of benediction” / OK sign</b> when making a fist (index &amp; middle can’t flex). Distal: <b>ape hand</b>, thenar atrophy, ↓ opposition. <b>AIN</b>: can’t make OK sign, no sensory loss</td><td>Palmar lateral 3½ digits (+ thenar palm if proximal)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Ulnar (C8–T1)</td><td>Medial epicondyle fracture (“funny bone”), Guyon canal (hook of hamate, cyclists)</td><td><b>Ulnar claw</b> (4th, 5th: MCP hyperextension, IP flexion) — worse with distal lesion (<b>ulnar paradox</b>: proximal lesion also paralyzes FDP to 4–5, less clawing); ↓ abduction/adduction of fingers; <b>Froment sign</b> (adductor pollicis); hypothenar &amp; 1st dorsal interosseous wasting</td><td>Medial 1½ digits, hypothenar</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Long thoracic (C5–7)</td><td>Mastectomy, axillary node dissection, stab</td><td>Winged scapula (medial), can’t abduct &gt;90°</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Recurrent branch of median</td><td>Superficial laceration of thenar eminence</td><td>Thenar paralysis, ↓ opposition</td><td>None</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"mn\">Claw hand: lumbricals lost → MCP extended, IP flexed. “Hand of benediction” appears when trying to make a fist (proximal median); ulnar claw appears at rest (distal ulnar).</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Breast &amp; axilla</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">~75% of breast lymph → axillary nodes</span> (pectoral/anterior first; sentinel node); medial breast → parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes. Suspensory (Cooper) ligaments → skin dimpling; lymphatic blockage → peau d’orange.</li>\n<li>Axilla walls: anterior (pec major/minor), posterior (subscapularis, teres major, latissimus), medial (serratus anterior, ribs), lateral (intertubercular groove). Contents: axillary artery/vein, brachial plexus cords, nodes.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Axillary artery divided by pectoralis minor into 3 parts</span>: 1st part: superior thoracic (1 branch); 2nd: thoracoacromial, lateral thoracic (2); 3rd: subscapular, anterior &amp; posterior circumflex humeral (3) <span class=\"mn\">(“Screw The Lawyer Save A Patient”)</span>.</li>\n<li>Axillary dissection risks: <b>long thoracic</b> (winging), <b>thoracodorsal</b> (weak latissimus), intercostobrachial (numb medial arm), lymphedema.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Upper limb blood supply</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Subclavian → (1st rib lateral border) axillary → (teres major inferior border) <b>brachial</b> → (cubital fossa) radial + ulnar.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Profunda brachii runs with the radial nerve in the radial groove</span> (midshaft fractures). Posterior circumflex humeral with axillary nerve (surgical neck).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Superficial palmar arch = mainly ulnar artery; deep palmar arch = mainly radial artery.</span> <b>Allen test</b> checks collateral flow before radial artery puncture/cannulation.</li>\n<li>Veins: cephalic (lateral, deltopectoral groove → axillary), basilic (medial → brachial/axillary), median cubital (connects; venipuncture).</li>\n<li>BP cuff over brachial artery; pulses: radial at wrist lateral to FCR tendon.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g8\">8. Gluteal region &amp; hip joint</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Nerve</th><th>Muscles</th><th>Lesion</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Superior gluteal (L4–S1)</td><td>Gluteus medius, minimus, TFL (abduct, medially rotate thigh; keep pelvis level)</td><td><b>Trendelenburg sign</b>: pelvis drops on the <b>opposite (unsupported) side</b> when standing on the affected limb; waddling gait. Cause: IM injection, hip surgery</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Inferior gluteal (L5–S2)</td><td>Gluteus maximus (extension, lateral rotation; climbing stairs, rising from a chair)</td><td>Difficulty climbing stairs, rising from seated; posterior lurch</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Piriformis = key landmark</span>: superior gluteal nerve/vessels exit <b>above</b> it; sciatic, inferior gluteal, pudendal nerves, posterior femoral cutaneous exit <b>below</b> it (greater sciatic foramen). Pudendal re-enters via lesser sciatic foramen (block near ischial spine).</li>\n<li>Lateral rotators: piriformis, superior/inferior gemelli, obturator internus (nerve to obturator internus), quadratus femoris, obturator externus (obturator n.).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">IM injection: superolateral quadrant of buttock (or ventrogluteal) to avoid sciatic nerve.</span></li>\n<li>Hip joint: ball-and-socket; <span class=\"hy\">iliofemoral ligament (Y of Bigelow) strongest, prevents hyperextension</span>; pubofemoral, ischiofemoral; ligament of head (artery of obturator origin, important in children).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Femoral head blood supply mainly medial circumflex femoral artery</span> (retinacular branches) → femoral neck fracture → <b>avascular necrosis</b>. Displaced fracture: limb shortened, laterally rotated.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Posterior hip dislocation</span> (dashboard injury, most common): limb shortened, <b>flexed, adducted, medially rotated</b>; sciatic nerve risk. Anterior: abducted, laterally rotated.</li>\n<li>Pediatric hip: DDH (Ortolani/Barlow), Legg–Calvé–Perthes (AVN, 4–10 y), SCFE (obese adolescent, knee pain, limp).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g9\">9. Thigh, femoral triangle &amp; adductor canal</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior compartment (femoral n.): quadriceps (knee extension; rectus femoris also flexes hip), sartorius, iliopsoas (strongest hip flexor). Medial (obturator n.): adductors, gracilis, (pectineus femoral). Posterior (tibial division of sciatic): hamstrings — semitendinosus, semimembranosus, biceps femoris long head (short head = common fibular).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Femoral triangle: inguinal ligament (superior), sartorius (lateral), adductor longus (medial). Contents lateral → medial: Nerve, Artery, Vein, (Empty space), Lymphatics</span> <span class=\"mn\">(NAVEL)</span>. <b>Femoral sheath</b> contains artery, vein and femoral canal (lymphatics) — NOT the nerve. <span class=\"hy\">Femoral hernia</span> through femoral canal, below and lateral to pubic tubercle, more common in women, high strangulation risk.</li>\n<li>Femoral artery pulse at mid-inguinal point; access for catheterization.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Adductor (Hunter) canal</span>: femoral artery, femoral vein, <b>saphenous nerve</b>, nerve to vastus medialis; roof = sartorius. Femoral vessels pass through the <b>adductor hiatus</b> → popliteal.</li>\n<li>Pes anserinus (medial tibia): Sartorius, Gracilis, semiTendinosus <span class=\"mn\">(“Say Grace before Tea”)</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g10\">10. Knee joint &amp; popliteal fossa</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Function / injury</th><th>Test</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>ACL (lateral femoral condyle → anterior tibia)</td><td>Prevents anterior tibial translation; non-contact pivot/twist</td><td>Anterior drawer, <b>Lachman</b></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>PCL (medial condyle → posterior tibia)</td><td>Prevents posterior translation; dashboard injury</td><td>Posterior drawer, sag sign</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>MCL</td><td>Resists valgus (blow to lateral knee); attached to medial meniscus</td><td>Valgus stress</td></tr>\n<tr><td>LCL</td><td>Resists varus; not attached to meniscus</td><td>Varus stress</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Medial meniscus</td><td>C-shaped, less mobile → injured more</td><td>McMurray; locking, joint-line pain</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">“Unhappy triad”: ACL + MCL + meniscus</span> (lateral blow to planted knee; classically medial meniscus, though lateral is more common acutely).</li>\n<li>Screw-home/locking; <b>popliteus unlocks</b> the knee (laterally rotates femur). Prepatellar bursitis (“housemaid’s knee”); Baker cyst (semimembranosus–gastrocnemius bursa).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Popliteal fossa</span>: borders biceps femoris (superolateral), semimembranosus/semitendinosus (superomedial), gastrocnemius heads (inferior). Contents superficial → deep: <b>tibial nerve, popliteal vein, popliteal artery</b> (deepest; aneurysm, injury in knee dislocation). Common fibular nerve along biceps tendon.</li>\n<li>Patellar dislocation usually lateral (vastus medialis oblique resists). Osgood–Schlatter = tibial tuberosity apophysitis.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g11\">11. Leg compartments</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Compartment</th><th>Muscles</th><th>Nerve</th><th>Artery</th><th>Action</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Anterior</td><td>Tibialis anterior, EHL, EDL, fibularis tertius</td><td>Deep fibular</td><td>Anterior tibial (→ dorsalis pedis)</td><td>Dorsiflexion, toe extension, inversion (TA)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Lateral</td><td>Fibularis longus, brevis</td><td>Superficial fibular</td><td>(Fibular artery branches)</td><td>Eversion</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Posterior superficial</td><td>Gastrocnemius, soleus, plantaris</td><td>Tibial</td><td>Posterior tibial</td><td>Plantarflexion</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Posterior deep</td><td>Tibialis posterior, FDL, FHL, popliteus</td><td>Tibial</td><td>Posterior tibial, fibular</td><td>Plantarflexion, inversion, toe flexion</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Anterior compartment syndrome</span> (tibial fracture, crush, reperfusion): pain out of proportion, pain on passive stretch, tense compartment; late pulselessness → fasciotomy. Shin splints = medial tibial stress.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Behind medial malleolus (tarsal tunnel): “Tom, Dick And Very Nervous Harry”</span> — Tibialis posterior, flexor Digitorum longus, posterior tibial Artery, Vein, tibial Nerve, flexor Hallucis longus. Pulse: posterior tibial artery here; dorsalis pedis lateral to EHL tendon on dorsum.</li>\n<li>Achilles (calcaneal) tendon rupture: pop, can’t plantarflex strongly; <b>Thompson test</b> positive.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g12\">12. Ankle &amp; foot</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Ankle (talocrural) = hinge: dorsiflexion/plantarflexion; <span class=\"hy\">most stable in dorsiflexion</span> (wider anterior talus). Inversion/eversion at <b>subtalar</b> and transverse tarsal joints.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Inversion sprain (most common): anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL) tears first</span>, then calcaneofibular. Deltoid ligament (medial) resists eversion — so strong that eversion injuries avulse the medial malleolus.</li>\n<li>Arches: medial longitudinal (keystone talus head/navicular; spring ligament, tibialis posterior, plantar aponeurosis), lateral longitudinal, transverse (fibularis longus).</li>\n<li>Plantar fasciitis (heel pain worst first steps in morning). Jones fracture (5th MT diaphysis, poor healing) vs avulsion of 5th MT tuberosity (fibularis brevis). March fracture (2nd MT). Lisfranc injury (tarsometatarsal).</li>\n<li>Sole: 4 layers; medial plantar nerve (≈ median) and lateral plantar nerve (≈ ulnar).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g13\">13. Lower limb nerves &amp; spinal levels — MUST KNOW</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Nerve</th><th>Injured by</th><th>Motor deficit</th><th>Sensory loss</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Common fibular (L4–S2)</td><td><b>Fibular neck fracture</b>, tight cast, leg crossing, lateral knee trauma</td><td><b>Foot drop</b> (↓ dorsiflexion), steppage gait, ↓ eversion; foot plantarflexed &amp; inverted <span class=\"mn\">(“PED = Peroneal Everts and Dorsiflexes; if injured, foot dropPED”)</span></td><td>Lateral leg, dorsum of foot</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Deep fibular</td><td>Anterior compartment syndrome</td><td>Foot drop (TA, EHL, EDL)</td><td><b>First web space</b> only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Superficial fibular</td><td>Lateral leg trauma</td><td>↓ eversion</td><td>Dorsum of foot (spares 1st web space)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Tibial (L4–S3)</td><td>Knee trauma, Baker cyst, tarsal tunnel</td><td>Can’t plantarflex/invert, can’t stand on toes; foot dorsiflexed &amp; everted <span class=\"mn\">(“TIP = Tibial Inverts and Plantarflexes; if injured, can’t stand on TIPtoes”)</span></td><td>Sole of foot</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Femoral (L2–L4)</td><td>Pelvic fracture, femoral catheterization, psoas hematoma</td><td>↓ knee extension, ↓ hip flexion; ↓ patellar reflex</td><td>Anterior thigh, medial leg (saphenous)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Obturator (L2–L4)</td><td>Pelvic surgery, labor, anterior hip dislocation</td><td>↓ thigh adduction</td><td>Medial thigh</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Superior gluteal (L4–S1)</td><td>IM injection, hip surgery</td><td>Trendelenburg sign (contralateral pelvic drop)</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Inferior gluteal (L5–S2)</td><td>Posterior hip dislocation</td><td>Can’t climb stairs / rise from seat</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Sciatic (L4–S3)</td><td>Posterior hip dislocation, misplaced IM injection</td><td>Hamstrings + all below-knee muscles</td><td>Most of leg and foot (not medial)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Lateral femoral cutaneous (L2–3)</td><td>Tight belts, obesity, pregnancy (at ASIS)</td><td>None</td><td><b>Meralgia paresthetica</b>: anterolateral thigh</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Disc herniation: usually posterolateral at L4–L5 or L5–S1 → compresses the nerve root BELOW</span> (L4–5 disc → L5 root: weak dorsiflexion/big toe extension, dorsum of foot numb; L5–S1 → S1: ↓ Achilles reflex, lateral foot). Positive straight-leg raise.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cauda equina syndrome</span>: saddle anesthesia, bowel/bladder dysfunction, bilateral sciatica → emergency.</li>\n<li>Lumbar puncture between L3–L4 or L4–L5 (iliac crest = L4 spinous process). Spinal cord ends L1–L2 in adults (conus medullaris).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g14\">14. Lower limb vessels &amp; lymphatics</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>External iliac → (under inguinal ligament) femoral → <b>profunda femoris</b> (medial &amp; lateral circumflex femoral; perforators) → femoral passes adductor hiatus → <b>popliteal</b> → anterior tibial (→ dorsalis pedis) + tibioperoneal trunk (posterior tibial + fibular).</li>\n<li>Pulses: femoral (mid-inguinal point), popliteal (deep, knee flexed), posterior tibial (behind medial malleolus), <b>dorsalis pedis</b> (lateral to EHL tendon).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Great saphenous vein: anterior to medial malleolus → medial leg/thigh → joins femoral vein at the saphenous opening</span> (CABG graft; saphenous nerve runs with it below knee). Small saphenous: behind lateral malleolus → popliteal vein (with sural nerve).</li>\n<li>DVT: deep veins of calf/popliteal/femoral → PE. Varicose veins: incompetent valves of superficial/perforator veins.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Lymph: superficial inguinal nodes drain lower limb (superficial), skin below umbilicus, perineum, external genitalia (not testes), anal canal below pectinate line, lower vagina.</span> Popliteal nodes drain the lateral foot/posterolateral leg (small saphenous territory). Testes → para-aortic nodes.</li>\n</ul>", "msk-embryology": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Origins of the musculoskeletal system</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Source</th><th>Gives rise to</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Paraxial mesoderm → somites (from week 3; ~42–44 pairs)</td><td><b>Sclerotome</b> → vertebrae, ribs, part of occipital bone; <b>myotome</b> → skeletal muscle of trunk &amp; limbs; <b>dermatome</b> → dermis of back</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Lateral plate mesoderm (somatic/parietal layer)</td><td>Limb bones, cartilage, tendons, ligaments, sternum, dermis of limbs/body wall</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Neural crest</td><td>Most of the face and skull vault (viscerocranium &amp; frontal/parietal parts), melanocytes, Schwann cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Notochord</td><td class=\"hy\">Nucleus pulposus of intervertebral disc; induces neural tube &amp; sclerotome</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Surface ectoderm</td><td>Epidermis, hair, nails, glands, AER of limb bud</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Cartilage &amp; bone formation</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Mesenchyme condenses → chondroblasts (SOX9) or osteoblasts (<b>RUNX2/CBFA1</b>, osterix).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Intramembranous ossification: mesenchyme → bone directly (no cartilage) — flat bones of the skull vault, face, mandible, clavicle (also first bone to ossify, week 5–6).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Endochondral ossification: hyaline cartilage model replaced by bone — long bones, vertebrae, pelvis, base of skull.</span> Steps: cartilage model → chondrocytes hypertrophy &amp; matrix calcifies → periosteal bone collar → nutrient artery invades (periosteal bud) → <b>primary ossification center in diaphysis</b> (by week 12) → <b>secondary centers in epiphyses</b> (mostly after birth; distal femur at birth) → epiphyseal plates remain until puberty ends.</li>\n<li>Cartilage growth: interstitial (from within) and appositional (from perichondrium).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cleidocranial dysplasia</span>: RUNX2 mutation (AD) → absent/hypoplastic clavicles (shoulders meet in midline), delayed fontanelle closure, wide sutures, supernumerary teeth — intramembranous bones affected.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Long bone growth</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Length = epiphyseal (growth) plate. Width = appositional growth from periosteum (osteoblasts add, osteoclasts remodel inner surface).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Growth plate zones (epiphysis → diaphysis): reserve/resting → proliferative (columns, stacked coins) → hypertrophic (maturation) → calcified cartilage → ossification.</span></li>\n<li>Regulators: GH/IGF-1 (proliferation), thyroid hormone, <b>estrogen closes the plates</b> (in both sexes — aromatase/estrogen receptor deficiency → tall stature, open plates), PTHrP/Indian hedgehog loop, FGFR3 (inhibits proliferation).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Achondroplasia</span>: activating FGFR3 mutation (AD; most cases new, ↑ paternal age) → ↓ chondrocyte proliferation in the growth plate → short limbs, normal trunk, large head, frontal bossing, normal intelligence; intramembranous bone normal.</li>\n<li>Rickets (vitamin D deficiency, children): defective mineralization at the hypertrophic zone → widened plates, bowed legs, rachitic rosary, craniotabes. Salter–Harris fractures involve the plate.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Gigantism</span> (↑ GH before plate closure) vs <b>acromegaly</b> (after closure — only appositional growth: hands, feet, jaw).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Vertebrae &amp; bone disorders</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Resegmentation</span>: each sclerotome splits; the caudal half of one fuses with the cranial half of the next → vertebral body is <b>intersegmental</b>; spinal nerves exit between vertebrae; myotomes bridge discs (so muscles move the spine).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Intervertebral disc: nucleus pulposus = notochord remnant; annulus fibrosus = sclerotome (fibrocartilage).</span> Herniation = nucleus through annulus (posterolateral).</li>\n<li>Neural arch closure defects: <span class=\"hy\">spina bifida occulta</span> (failure of vertebral arch fusion, no herniation; tuft of hair/dimple at L5–S1; normal AFP), <b>meningocele</b> (meninges herniate), <b>myelomeningocele</b> (meninges + cord; ↑ AFP; Arnold–Chiari II). Folate prevents NTDs.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Klippel–Feil syndrome</span>: fusion of cervical vertebrae → short neck, low hairline, limited neck motion.</li>\n<li><b>Hemivertebra</b> (failure of half a vertebra to form) → congenital scoliosis. Scoliosis (lateral), kyphosis (thoracic hump), lordosis (lumbar).</li>\n<li>Cervical rib (from C7) → thoracic outlet syndrome. Lumbar rib, sacralization of L5 / lumbarization of S1.</li>\n<li>Primary curvatures (thoracic, sacral — fetal) vs secondary (cervical when holding head up, lumbar when walking).</li>\n<li>Other congenital bone disorders: <b>osteogenesis imperfecta</b> (type I collagen, blue sclerae, fractures), <b>osteopetrosis</b> (osteoclast failure, dense brittle bones), <b>Marfan</b> (fibrillin-1), <b>arthrogryposis</b> (multiple joint contractures from ↓ fetal movement/oligohydramnios).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Skull, sutures &amp; fontanelles</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Neurocranium</span> (encloses brain): <b>membranous part</b> = flat bones of the vault (intramembranous; neural crest &amp; paraxial mesoderm); <b>cartilaginous part (chondrocranium)</b> = base of skull (endochondral). <b>Viscerocranium</b> = face (mainly neural crest from pharyngeal arches 1–2).</li>\n<li>Sutures (dense CT) allow molding at birth and brain growth. <span class=\"hy\">Anterior fontanelle (diamond; frontal–parietal junction) closes ~18–24 months; posterior fontanelle (triangular) closes ~2–3 months.</span> Bulging fontanelle → ↑ ICP; sunken → dehydration.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Craniosynostosis</span> = premature suture closure → skull grows parallel to the fused suture:\n<ul><li class=\"hy\"><b>Sagittal</b> (most common, males) → <b>scaphocephaly / dolichocephaly</b> (long, narrow head).</li>\n<li><b>Coronal</b> (bilateral) → brachycephaly (short, wide; Apert/Crouzon — FGFR2); unilateral → anterior plagiocephaly.</li>\n<li><b>Lambdoid</b> (unilateral) → posterior plagiocephaly (vs positional plagiocephaly from sleeping position — far more common).</li>\n<li><b>Metopic</b> → trigonocephaly (triangular forehead).</li>\n<li>Multiple sutures → oxycephaly/turricephaly (tower skull), cloverleaf skull (FGFR).</li></ul></li>\n<li>Microcephaly (small brain → small skull; Zika, alcohol) vs anencephaly (failure of anterior neuropore — no vault).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Limb development</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Limb buds appear end of week 4 (upper slightly before lower); key period weeks 4–8</span> — most limb defects (e.g., <b>thalidomide → phocomelia/amelia</b>, days 24–36).</li>\n<li>Bud = lateral plate mesoderm core (bones, CT) covered by ectoderm; <b>muscle from somite myotomes</b> migrates in (dorsal/ventral muscle masses → extensors/flexors).</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Signaling center</th><th>Molecule</th><th>Axis / role</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Apical ectodermal ridge (AER)</td><td>FGFs (FGF8)</td><td>Proximal–distal outgrowth; removal → truncated limb</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Zone of polarizing activity (ZPA), posterior</td><td>Sonic hedgehog (SHH)</td><td>Anterior–posterior (thumb ↔ little finger); ectopic anterior ZPA → mirror duplication / polydactyly</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dorsal ectoderm</td><td>WNT7a</td><td>Dorsal–ventral (nails dorsal)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HOX genes</td><td>—</td><td>Segment identity; mutations → limb malformations (synpolydactyly)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Digits separate by apoptosis</span> in interdigital regions (weeks 6–8; BMPs) → failure = <b>syndactyly</b>. Extra digits = polydactyly.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Limb rotation (week 7)</span>: upper limb rotates <b>90° laterally</b> (extensors posterior, thumb lateral); lower limb rotates <b>90° medially</b> (extensors anterior, big toe medial) → dermatomes spiral in the leg.</li>\n<li>Defects: amelia (absent limb), meromelia (partial), phocomelia (hands/feet attached close to trunk), clubfoot (talipes equinovarus — most common; inverted, plantarflexed), congenital hip dysplasia, amniotic band syndrome (constriction rings/amputations).</li>\n<li>Joints: interzones of mesenchyme → synovial joints (cavity by apoptosis); fibrous and cartilaginous joints form where mesenchyme differentiates.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Muscle development</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Skeletal muscle: from paraxial mesoderm (somite myotomes)</span>; head muscles from somitomeres/pharyngeal arch mesoderm. Myoblasts (MyoD, Myf5, myogenin) <b>fuse</b> → multinucleated myotubes → fibers; satellite cells remain as stem cells.</li>\n<li><b>Epimere</b> (dorsal) → deep back extensors (dorsal rami); <b>hypomere</b> (ventral) → body wall &amp; limb muscles (ventral rami).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Smooth muscle: splanchnic (visceral) lateral plate mesoderm</span> (gut, vessels); iris sphincter/dilator from neuroectoderm. <b>Cardiac</b>: splanchnic mesoderm around the heart tube.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Poland sequence</span>: absent pectoralis major (sternocostal head) ± ipsilateral syndactyly/breast hypoplasia. <b>Prune belly</b> syndrome: absent abdominal wall muscles. Congenital torticollis (SCM fibrosis).</li>\n<li><b>Duchenne/Becker</b> (dystrophin) — X-linked; Gowers sign; covered in histology/cell bio.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g8\">8. Integumentary system</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Epidermis = surface ectoderm; dermis = mesoderm (dermatome on back, lateral plate elsewhere); melanocytes = neural crest</span> (migrate by weeks 8–12). Langerhans cells = bone marrow; Merkel cells = neural crest.</li>\n<li>Epidermis starts as a single layer + <b>periderm</b> (shed into amniotic fluid → <b>vernix caseosa</b> with sebum); stratified by ~week 21.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Hair follicles</span>: epidermal downgrowth into dermis; <b>lanugo</b> (fine fetal hair, shed before/after birth) replaced by vellus/terminal hair. Hair papilla = mesoderm.</li>\n<li>Glands (all from epidermis): sebaceous (from follicle wall), sweat glands, <b>mammary glands</b> (modified sweat glands along the milk line → supernumerary nipples = polythelia).</li>\n<li>Nails start ~week 10 at fingertips (reach tips ~32 weeks; toes later — useful for gestational age).</li>\n<li>Teeth: enamel = ectoderm (ameloblasts); dentin/pulp = neural crest (odontoblasts).</li>\n<li>Disorders: <span class=\"hy\">albinism</span> (normal melanocyte number, ↓ tyrosinase/melanin), vitiligo (↓ melanocytes, autoimmune), <b>ichthyosis</b> (abnormal keratinization; harlequin), <b>ectodermal dysplasia</b> (absent sweat glands, sparse hair, abnormal teeth), epidermolysis bullosa (anchoring proteins: keratin, collagen VII), hemangiomas.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g9\">9. Rapid-fire NBME pearls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">Intramembranous: skull vault, face, clavicle. Endochondral: long bones, vertebrae, skull base.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Achondroplasia = FGFR3 (proliferative zone); cleidocranial dysplasia = RUNX2.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Nucleus pulposus from notochord; vertebrae from sclerotome by resegmentation.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Sagittal synostosis → scaphocephaly (long narrow head); anterior fontanelle closes by ~18–24 months.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">AER = FGF (length); ZPA = SHH (anterior–posterior, polydactyly); digits separate by apoptosis (syndactyly).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Upper limb rotates laterally, lower limb medially; limb buds week 4; thalidomide → phocomelia.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Skeletal muscle = myotome (paraxial mesoderm); smooth = splanchnic mesoderm; melanocytes = neural crest.</li>\n</ul>"};
